981
00:40:56,938 --> 00:40:57,938
What's going on?

982
00:40:59,578 --> 00:41:01,208
- It says "potato." Gosh.
- What?

983
00:41:01,208 --> 00:41:02,918
- Oh, no.
- What's going on?

984
00:41:05,548 --> 00:41:07,748
I'm wearing slippers.

985
00:41:08,306 --> 00:41:10,046
- It says "potato." Gosh.
- What?

986
00:41:10,046 --> 00:41:11,746
- Oh, no.
- What's going on?

987
00:41:14,016 --> 00:41:16,346
- What?
- I have slippers on.

988
00:41:17,046 --> 00:41:18,516
- What?
- What is that?

989
00:41:18,516 --> 00:41:19,816
Weren't we going to the village hall?

990
00:41:19,816 --> 00:41:20,986
(What happened to the onion and potato expedition?)

991
00:41:20,986 --> 00:41:22,256
- Potatoes.
- Potatoes?

992
00:41:23,886 --> 00:41:24,886
What's going on?

993
00:41:24,886 --> 00:41:26,096
(2 cars appear in front of the onion and potato expedition.)

994
00:41:26,656 --> 00:41:29,426
- What?
- I have slippers on.

995
00:41:31,666 --> 00:41:33,896
(On each car is...)

996
00:41:34,736 --> 00:41:37,506
(Potato)

997
00:41:38,366 --> 00:41:42,036
(Onion)

998
00:41:42,346 --> 00:41:43,476
- What's that?
- Weren't we going to...

999
00:41:43,476 --> 00:41:44,646
the village hall?

1000
00:41:44,646 --> 00:41:46,316
I have slippers on, okay?

1001
00:41:46,316 --> 00:41:47,776
I'm digging out potatoes all alone.

1002
00:41:48,276 --> 00:41:49,416
What's going on here?

1003
00:41:50,546 --> 00:41:52,456
What? Weren't we going to the village hall?

1004
00:41:54,556 --> 00:41:56,126
Wasn't this the village hall?

1005
00:41:56,126 --> 00:41:57,226
Get in.

1006
00:41:57,486 --> 00:41:58,856
Isn't this the village hall?

1007
00:41:59,226 --> 00:42:00,256
Are we getting in?

1008
00:42:00,256 --> 00:42:01,896
I'm all alone.

1009
00:42:02,426 --> 00:42:04,266
Wait. You told us to dress up.

1010
00:42:05,066 --> 00:42:06,336
I'm wearing jeans.

1011
00:42:06,436 --> 00:42:08,166
Get in, onion and potato.

1012
00:42:08,566 --> 00:42:09,566
I'm not fine.

1013
00:42:09,566 --> 00:42:11,406
(Crying out at the unbelievable reality)

1014
00:42:11,866 --> 00:42:14,106
- Let me put on some sunscreen.
- I get all shy when I'm alone.

1015
00:42:14,106 --> 00:42:15,706
Did we do something wrong?

1016
00:42:15,706 --> 00:42:16,706
Why do you keep...

1017
00:42:17,876 --> 00:42:19,046
tricking us?

1018
00:42:19,046 --> 00:42:20,046
(At the verge of crying)

1019
00:42:20,476 --> 00:42:21,616
Jung Ah!

1020
00:42:21,976 --> 00:42:24,016
- Jung Ah!
- Jung Ah!

1021
00:42:24,016 --> 00:42:25,446
(The youngsters cry out to Jung Ah.)

1022
00:42:26,086 --> 00:42:28,486
Save us, Jung Ah!

1023
00:42:30,786 --> 00:42:32,696
(But the eldest wouldn't know about it.)

1024
00:42:37,266 --> 00:42:38,996
I don't want to go alone.

1025
00:42:39,996 --> 00:42:41,036
You'll be fine, right?

1026
00:42:41,036 --> 00:42:42,066
(Worried)

1027
00:42:42,066 --> 00:42:43,636
I hate going alone the most.

1028
00:42:43,636 --> 00:42:45,976
(You Seok gets in the potato car alone.)

1029
00:42:47,436 --> 00:42:49,176
Let's turn all the cameras around.

1030
00:42:49,246 --> 00:42:50,276
For real.

1031
00:42:50,276 --> 00:42:52,416
Calm down. Once we get to the onion field,

1032
00:42:52,946 --> 00:42:54,546
there will be elders there.

1033
00:42:55,146 --> 00:42:58,616
Greet them, and then bring the onions back in our baskets.

1034
00:42:58,616 --> 00:43:00,886
My gut is telling me we have to dig up the onions.

1035
00:43:00,956 --> 00:43:02,086
No way.

1036
00:43:02,086 --> 00:43:03,526
(Sun Young is anxious about the sudden potential work.)

1037
00:43:04,526 --> 00:43:06,096
(You Seok puts on sunscreen, expecting work.)

1038
00:43:06,096 --> 00:43:08,456
(Rubbing it in)

1039
00:43:08,456 --> 00:43:10,496
This is just hilarious now.

1040
00:43:11,266 --> 00:43:12,436
(Laughing)

1041
00:43:13,536 --> 00:43:15,536
(Laughing)

1042
00:43:15,566 --> 00:43:16,766
(Unusual behaviour from extreme anxiety)

1043
00:43:16,766 --> 00:43:18,106
This reminds me of the boat.

1044
00:43:18,806 --> 00:43:21,006
(Applying)

1045
00:43:21,736 --> 00:43:22,876
Okay, I've got it now.

1046
00:43:23,706 --> 00:43:25,876
I've got the hang of Fresh off the Sea now.

1047
00:43:25,876 --> 00:43:27,146
(Gotten the hang of Fresh off the Sea)

1048
00:43:27,476 --> 00:43:28,646
It's...

1049
00:43:29,616 --> 00:43:30,946
Really.

1050
00:43:30,946 --> 00:43:32,516
(At a loss for words)

1051
00:43:32,686 --> 00:43:33,986
Just when I put my guard down,

1052
00:43:35,756 --> 00:43:37,186
there's a surprise event.

1053
00:43:38,656 --> 00:43:39,996
(Sighing)

1054
00:43:40,626 --> 00:43:42,526
Tell us where we're going at the least.

1055
00:43:42,526 --> 00:43:44,666
We're heading to dig up onions. The onions are...

1056
00:43:44,666 --> 00:43:47,196
Are we going to dig them up or simply get them?

1057
00:43:47,196 --> 00:43:48,336
We could go to a supermarket.

1058
00:43:48,336 --> 00:43:49,666
- Dig them up.
- Dig them up.

1059
00:43:50,066 --> 00:43:51,436
Am I going alone to dig up potatoes?

1060
00:43:54,036 --> 00:43:55,206
(Onion, Potato)

1061
00:43:55,206 --> 00:43:58,776
(The two cars part ways to different destinations.)

1062
00:44:01,016 --> 00:44:02,276
How long will it take?

1063
00:44:02,846 --> 00:44:05,516
It'll take over 30 minutes.

1064
00:44:05,716 --> 00:44:06,916
Thirty minutes for real?

1065
00:44:06,916 --> 00:44:08,856
(The onion expedition heads to Geoje Island.)

1066
00:44:08,856 --> 00:44:11,556
I always bring my bag with me.

1067
00:44:11,556 --> 00:44:13,356
But I didn't bring one this time because they said...

1068
00:44:13,926 --> 00:44:15,926
- we'd be going to the village hall.
- I won't believe anyone now.

1069
00:44:15,926 --> 00:44:18,226
I'll only doubt things more.

1070
00:44:18,366 --> 00:44:19,366
(They were caught completely off guard again.)

1071
00:44:19,366 --> 00:44:20,836
Right. I let my guard down.

1072
00:44:21,696 --> 00:44:22,766
(Laughing)

1073
00:44:22,766 --> 00:44:23,866
We've got kidnapped again.

1074
00:44:23,866 --> 00:44:25,136
I have nothing to say.

1075
00:44:25,136 --> 00:44:26,536
After all, I didn't ride a boat.

1076
00:44:27,506 --> 00:44:29,276
- You... True.
- Right?

1077
00:44:29,276 --> 00:44:31,106
This annoys me the most. The onion sign.

1078
00:44:31,106 --> 00:44:33,946
This sign that's put up sloppy...

1079
00:44:33,946 --> 00:44:35,416
It's fluttering in the wind.

1080
00:44:36,216 --> 00:44:37,646
(Dumbfounded)

1081
00:44:37,646 --> 00:44:38,716
I'm taking a photo of it.

1082
00:44:40,056 --> 00:44:41,856
(Onion)

1083
00:44:41,856 --> 00:44:43,126
(Fluttering)

1084
00:44:43,126 --> 00:44:44,326
"Onion."

1085
00:44:44,326 --> 00:44:46,956
(A design that looks like they didn't even bother with it)

1086
00:44:47,396 --> 00:44:48,426
How annoying.

1087
00:44:50,766 --> 00:44:52,796
(Laughing)

1088
00:44:55,936 --> 00:44:58,906
(Acting strangely)

1089
00:44:58,906 --> 00:45:02,476
Does Jung Ah know about what's going on?

1090
00:45:02,476 --> 00:45:03,606
She'll find out later.

1091
00:45:03,606 --> 00:45:05,046
So she has no idea yet?

1092
00:45:05,476 --> 00:45:07,176
(Shocked)

1093
00:45:07,176 --> 00:45:08,416
How will she find out later?

1094
00:45:09,416 --> 00:45:10,846
I get it now.

1095
00:45:10,986 --> 00:45:14,256
Once we return from work,

1096
00:45:14,556 --> 00:45:17,026
Jung Ah will have cooked the curry.

1097
00:45:17,486 --> 00:45:19,526
Isn't that your wish, Hae Joon?

1098
00:45:19,526 --> 00:45:21,056
It's not a wish...

1099
00:45:21,056 --> 00:45:22,526
but how things roll.

1100
00:45:24,066 --> 00:45:28,236
(It will fade away someday)

1101
00:45:28,236 --> 00:45:32,266
(Acting strangely again)

1102
00:45:32,366 --> 00:45:34,506
You Seok is off alone to dig up potatoes.

1103
00:45:34,506 --> 00:45:35,536
(Curious about how the Potato Guy is doing)

1104
00:45:35,536 --> 00:45:36,946
He must be complaining nonstop, right?

1105
00:45:38,176 --> 00:45:40,176
You Seok, all alone.

1106
00:45:40,676 --> 00:45:43,716
You Seok lost...

1107
00:45:43,716 --> 00:45:46,286
- when it was 3 to 1.
- Potatoes.

1108
00:45:46,286 --> 00:45:48,186
He had the worst luck in the draw this time.

1109
00:45:48,186 --> 00:45:49,486
(Feeling bad)

1110
00:45:50,486 --> 00:45:51,556
Someone is calling me.

1111
00:45:53,226 --> 00:45:54,496
It's a video call.

1112
00:45:54,826 --> 00:45:56,466
(A video call from the Potato Guy)

1113
00:45:56,466 --> 00:45:57,496
You Seok.

1114
00:45:58,296 --> 00:45:59,596
- Look, Jung Ah.
- Jung Ah!

1115
00:45:59,596 --> 00:46:00,936
They're being kidnapped.

1116
00:46:01,466 --> 00:46:03,366
- Why are you there, You Seok?
- Why are you there?

1117
00:46:03,836 --> 00:46:05,006
(Laughing)

1118
00:46:05,006 --> 00:46:06,506
- Why are you there, You Seok?
- What?

1119
00:46:06,506 --> 00:46:07,576
What's going on?

1120
00:46:08,706 --> 00:46:10,876
(You Seok smiling all brightly)

1121
00:46:12,646 --> 00:46:15,476
(He's with Jung Ah?)

1122
00:46:17,616 --> 00:46:20,516
(Everyone is totally puzzled.)

1123
00:46:20,516 --> 00:46:22,756
Wait, why is he there? The potatoes...

1124
00:46:22,756 --> 00:46:24,456
What's this? What about the potatoes?

1125
00:46:24,456 --> 00:46:26,596
- Potatoes...
- I already dug up the potatoes.

1126
00:46:27,796 --> 00:46:28,896
You dug up the potatoes?

1127
00:46:29,296 --> 00:46:30,866
I did. I already dug up potatoes.

1128
00:46:32,066 --> 00:46:35,036
(You Seok already dug up the potatoes?)

1129
00:46:36,206 --> 00:46:37,336
Really.

1130
00:46:37,336 --> 00:46:38,436
(10 minutes ago in the potato car)

1131
00:46:38,736 --> 00:46:40,876
Why are you doing this to me?

1132
00:46:40,876 --> 00:46:42,546
Why did I draw the potato?

1133
00:46:42,546 --> 00:46:45,646
Hae Joon should've drawn the potato.

1134
00:46:45,646 --> 00:46:46,876
(You Seok leaves alone in the potato car.)

1135
00:46:47,616 --> 00:46:48,816
(Laughing)

1136
00:46:48,816 --> 00:46:50,316
(Glancing)

1137
00:46:51,816 --> 00:46:52,856
What?

1138
00:46:53,356 --> 00:46:54,856
(Looking around)

1139
00:46:54,856 --> 00:46:55,886
What?

1140
00:46:58,926 --> 00:47:02,626
(The potato car returns to the Siblings' House.)

1141
00:47:04,866 --> 00:47:05,936
What's going on?

1142
00:47:06,296 --> 00:47:07,696
Good work. You can get out now.

1143
00:47:07,696 --> 00:47:08,796
(What?)

1144
00:47:08,796 --> 00:47:10,966
What?

1145
00:47:11,966 --> 00:47:12,976
(You Seok gathers his belongings in a rush.)

1146
00:47:12,976 --> 00:47:14,006
What?

1147
00:47:15,606 --> 00:47:16,676
What?

1148
00:47:17,546 --> 00:47:21,616
(Getting off fast)

1149
00:47:22,246 --> 00:47:26,386
(Surprised)

1150
00:47:26,386 --> 00:47:28,686
(Laughing)

1151
00:47:28,686 --> 00:47:32,186
(You Seok comes running with something in the basket.)

1152
00:47:32,186 --> 00:47:33,196
I'm saved.

1153
00:47:35,126 --> 00:47:36,196
(Inside You Seok's basket)

1154
00:47:36,196 --> 00:47:37,266
Jung Ah!

1155
00:47:40,836 --> 00:47:43,236
(Potatoes)

1156
00:47:43,336 --> 00:47:44,406
Jung Ah.

1157
00:47:44,536 --> 00:47:45,906
(You Seok gets a free pass from working!)

1158
00:47:45,906 --> 00:47:46,976
Jung Ah.

1159
00:47:48,536 --> 00:47:49,546
Where is Jung Ah?

1160
00:47:51,276 --> 00:47:53,976
Jung Ah, come out for a bit. This is bad.

1161
00:47:53,976 --> 00:47:55,476
(He must let Jung Ah know about this news!)

1162
00:47:55,476 --> 00:47:56,546
Jung Ah.

1163
00:47:57,516 --> 00:47:58,516
(Opening)

1164
00:47:58,516 --> 00:47:59,616
She was in the bathroom.

1165
00:47:59,616 --> 00:48:00,786
- What?
- I called you.

1166
00:48:01,056 --> 00:48:02,086
What for?

1167
00:48:02,356 --> 00:48:04,486
Sit for a minute. I have a funny story to tell.

1168
00:48:05,426 --> 00:48:06,526
Seriously, I...

1169
00:48:08,326 --> 00:48:10,926
We were heading down, playing pretend and stuff.

1170
00:48:11,226 --> 00:48:13,796
They said the village hall was down there, and we got there.

1171
00:48:14,866 --> 00:48:16,836
But suddenly, two cars drove in.

1172
00:48:17,966 --> 00:48:20,476
One potato car. One onion car.

1173
00:48:20,476 --> 00:48:21,536
Did they get kidnapped?

1174
00:48:21,676 --> 00:48:24,606
I was kidnapped, and so was the onion team.

1175
00:48:24,606 --> 00:48:26,146
I was alone in the car.

1176
00:48:26,146 --> 00:48:28,516
"I can't do it alone. There won't be enough airtime."

1177
00:48:28,516 --> 00:48:29,916
"It's hard for me." I kept grumbling.

1178
00:48:29,916 --> 00:48:32,516
But after a drive around the neighbourhood,

1179
00:48:32,516 --> 00:48:35,116
I was told to get off with the potatoes. I was saved.

1180
00:48:35,286 --> 00:48:36,586
- And...
- Have the guys been kidnapped?

1181
00:48:36,586 --> 00:48:38,356
They went to dig up onions.

1182
00:48:39,326 --> 00:48:41,126
Mine were bought. I was all alone.

1183
00:48:41,126 --> 00:48:42,866
I kept saying I couldn't go alone.

1184
00:48:42,866 --> 00:48:45,266
"I can't go." I kept saying that.

1185
00:48:45,266 --> 00:48:46,336
(How did you get here?)

1186
00:48:46,496 --> 00:48:47,566
I was saved all alone.

1187
00:48:47,566 --> 00:48:48,796
I can't believe you right now.

1188
00:48:48,796 --> 00:48:50,206
No, believe me.

1189
00:48:50,206 --> 00:48:51,336
I even called you...

1190
00:48:51,336 --> 00:48:53,136
after getting kidnapped. I video-called you.

1191
00:48:56,046 --> 00:48:57,276
- What is it, Jung Ah?
- Lock the door!

1192
00:48:59,046 --> 00:49:00,216
Where are you going, Jung Ah?

1193
00:49:00,546 --> 00:49:02,346
- You're being strange right now.
- Listen.

1194
00:49:02,746 --> 00:49:04,086
- You're too hyper.
- I...

1195
00:49:04,086 --> 00:49:05,846
It's because I was kidnapped earlier.

1196
00:49:05,846 --> 00:49:07,186
- No.
- Jung Ah, I...

1197
00:49:07,186 --> 00:49:08,816
- Jung Ah.
- I can't believe you.

1198
00:49:09,526 --> 00:49:10,826
Come over here, Jung Ah.

1199
00:49:10,826 --> 00:49:12,056
I wouldn't kidnap you, would I?

1200
00:49:12,126 --> 00:49:13,256
Come, Jung Ah.

1201
00:49:13,256 --> 00:49:15,426
Let's try calling Yoon Seo.

1202
00:49:15,626 --> 00:49:16,726
I have to cook.

1203
00:49:17,096 --> 00:49:18,626
Seriously, Jung Ah.

1204
00:49:18,626 --> 00:49:19,666
Why can't you believe me?

1205
00:49:19,666 --> 00:49:21,166
- Of course, I can't.
- How come?

1206
00:49:21,736 --> 00:49:22,836
Why not?

1207
00:49:24,166 --> 00:49:25,406
- Whatever.
- I'll prove it to you.

1208
00:49:25,406 --> 00:49:26,406
Call Yoon Seo.

1209
00:49:26,406 --> 00:49:27,536
(A deep mistrust sparked by the wasabi incident)

1210
00:49:27,936 --> 00:49:29,306
Let's call Yoon Seo as she's getting kidnapped.

1211
00:49:29,306 --> 00:49:30,306
(Feeling wronged)

1212
00:49:30,306 --> 00:49:31,446
If only it weren't for that wasabi incident.

1213
00:49:33,746 --> 00:49:35,216
- What...
- We're going to...

1214
00:49:35,216 --> 00:49:36,716
Geoje Island right now!

1215
00:49:37,216 --> 00:49:39,616
- What?
- We're going to Geoje Island!

1216
00:49:40,156 --> 00:49:42,286
The potato was the good one.

1217
00:49:42,656 --> 00:49:44,556
Gosh, this is really...

1218
00:49:44,656 --> 00:49:47,526
What a series of twists.

1219
00:49:47,526 --> 00:49:48,756
Fresh off the Sea is no joke.

1220
00:49:48,756 --> 00:49:50,896
This isn't the kind of Fresh off the Sea I knew.

1221
00:49:52,096 --> 00:49:54,066
Let's video call at the onion field.

1222
00:49:54,066 --> 00:49:55,666
I'll call while digging up onions.

1223
00:49:55,666 --> 00:49:57,036
(After the call with the Potato Guy)

1224
00:49:57,766 --> 00:49:59,306
(In despair)

1225
00:50:00,336 --> 00:50:02,536
Good. I'm rather happy.

1226
00:50:03,406 --> 00:50:04,576
Earlier, I was like,

1227
00:50:04,576 --> 00:50:06,676
"What's going on?"

1228
00:50:06,676 --> 00:50:09,146
"Why draw papers when there are five of us?"

1229
00:50:09,646 --> 00:50:10,686
I had my questions.

1230
00:50:11,016 --> 00:50:13,286
- The production staff was thorough.
- Yes.

1231
00:50:14,216 --> 00:50:16,026
Imagine how excited they must have been.

1232
00:50:17,156 --> 00:50:19,086
(Spaced out)

1233
00:50:19,086 --> 00:50:20,996
If I knew the car ride would be this long...

1234
00:50:20,996 --> 00:50:23,026
Just imagine how ridiculous we must have been to them.

1235
00:50:23,026 --> 00:50:25,226
- Like, "They don't know a thing." - We must've been funny to them.

1236
00:50:25,426 --> 00:50:27,166
Don't film us!

1237
00:50:27,736 --> 00:50:28,896
Don't film us!

1238
00:50:28,896 --> 00:50:29,936
(Production staff)

1239
00:50:29,936 --> 00:50:31,706
- What?
- Don't film us!

1240
00:50:32,666 --> 00:50:34,006
(Onion)

1241
00:50:34,006 --> 00:50:35,006
(Don't film us!)

1242
00:50:36,606 --> 00:50:37,606
(Don't film us!)

1243
00:50:37,776 --> 00:50:39,076
(How could we not?)

1244
00:50:39,076 --> 00:50:40,176
(Don't film us!)

1245
00:50:42,116 --> 00:50:43,646
(Haranguing)

1246
00:50:43,846 --> 00:50:45,116
(Laughing)

1247
00:50:45,116 --> 00:50:46,116
What?

1248
00:50:46,116 --> 00:50:47,156
(Breathing out heavily)

1249
00:50:47,316 --> 00:50:49,926
The production staff is filming us with a phone while smiling!

1250
00:50:50,126 --> 00:50:52,256
They look happy...

1251
00:50:52,256 --> 00:50:54,556
when we look all stressed out.

1252
00:50:55,756 --> 00:50:57,826
(Fluttering)

1253
00:50:57,926 --> 00:50:59,196
What chaos.

1254
00:51:00,336 --> 00:51:01,396
Should we take it off?

1255
00:51:01,936 --> 00:51:03,006
Yes.

1256
00:51:03,106 --> 00:51:07,006
(Taking it down immediately)

1257
00:51:07,276 --> 00:51:08,936
As expected of an art major. Nice.

1258
00:51:09,376 --> 00:51:10,776
- You're...
- How...

1259
00:51:11,046 --> 00:51:12,046
quick to act.

1260
00:51:14,776 --> 00:51:17,446
(Meanwhile at the Siblings' House)

1261
00:51:18,846 --> 00:51:21,016
- You believe me now, right?
- No.

1262
00:51:21,756 --> 00:51:23,126
I'll try to calm down.

1263
00:51:23,126 --> 00:51:24,826
Earlier, I was excited.

1264
00:51:24,886 --> 00:51:27,756
- Were you?
- The happiness that I was saved.

1265
00:51:27,826 --> 00:51:30,626
I went back and forth from paradise to the pit.

1266
00:51:30,626 --> 00:51:32,466
- I see.
- So open the door for me.

1267
00:51:32,466 --> 00:51:33,866
No, stay in your room.

1268
00:51:33,866 --> 00:51:35,136
- I'll stay here.
- Let's talk in the living room.

1269
00:51:35,336 --> 00:51:36,966
Shouldn't we talk in the living room...

1270
00:51:37,166 --> 00:51:38,636
- and secure airtime?
- You.

1271
00:51:38,636 --> 00:51:40,776
You're trying to kidnap me, aren't you?

1272
00:51:41,076 --> 00:51:42,136
I'll just...

1273
00:51:43,046 --> 00:51:44,906
(Screaming)

1274
00:51:44,906 --> 00:51:46,146
(Scared to death)

1275
00:51:46,446 --> 00:51:49,546
You should trust me, Jung Ah. Aren't we a family?

1276
00:51:49,546 --> 00:51:51,816
Why are you trying so hard to be with me?

1277
00:51:51,816 --> 00:51:53,686
- Just why?
- Being alone is lonely.

1278
00:51:54,086 --> 00:51:56,286
Let's hang out together. Come.

1279
00:51:57,056 --> 00:51:58,526
He's being weird.

1280
00:51:58,856 --> 00:52:00,396
(She still can't trust him.)

1281
00:52:00,856 --> 00:52:02,556
Please open the door, Jung Ah.

1282
00:52:02,866 --> 00:52:05,126
Why do you want to be with me?

1283
00:52:05,326 --> 00:52:06,936
Go and sleep in your room.

1284
00:52:07,296 --> 00:52:08,436
Jung Ah, we...

1285
00:52:08,436 --> 00:52:10,006
- Can I really just sleep?
- Yes.

1286
00:52:10,266 --> 00:52:11,436
Can I?

1287
00:52:11,706 --> 00:52:14,276
Go to your room. I have to cook.

1288
00:52:15,036 --> 00:52:16,676
- I'm going to my room.
- Okay.

1289
00:52:16,976 --> 00:52:18,546
(Giving up)

1290
00:52:18,676 --> 00:52:20,476
- I'm going to sleep.
- Okay.

1291
00:52:21,376 --> 00:52:22,376
(Closing)

1292
00:52:22,376 --> 00:52:24,116
(After hearing You Seok's room door close)

1293
00:52:25,416 --> 00:52:27,856
(Glancing)

1294
00:52:28,016 --> 00:52:30,656
(Window)

1295
00:52:30,656 --> 00:52:31,756
(Sealed shut)

1296
00:52:31,756 --> 00:52:32,896
Let's lock it.

1297
00:52:34,656 --> 00:52:37,096
(After locking the window)

1298
00:52:37,926 --> 00:52:39,736
(Opening)

1299
00:52:39,866 --> 00:52:42,396
(Carefully scanning the living room)

1300
00:52:43,236 --> 00:52:45,206
(After checking that You Seok's room door is shut)

1301
00:52:46,066 --> 00:52:49,436
(Quickly heading to the kitchen)

1302
00:52:51,376 --> 00:52:54,816
(Appearing)

1303
00:52:55,676 --> 00:52:57,346
(You Seok was watching the whole thing.)

1304
00:52:58,346 --> 00:53:00,556
(Jung Ah has no idea about it.)

1305
00:53:01,286 --> 00:53:03,056
(He's off to meet her now.)

1306
00:53:08,156 --> 00:53:09,156
Jung Ah.

1307
00:53:09,696 --> 00:53:10,866
You scared me.

1308
00:53:11,426 --> 00:53:12,426
Jung Ah.

1309
00:53:15,066 --> 00:53:16,906
(Dopamine level at its max)

1310
00:53:17,166 --> 00:53:18,206
Jung Ah.

1311
00:53:19,066 --> 00:53:20,376
What is it?

1312
00:53:20,676 --> 00:53:22,376
- I swear it's nothing.
- Right?

1313
00:53:22,376 --> 00:53:24,146
Either way, I need to cook.

1314
00:53:24,146 --> 00:53:25,506
Let's talk for a bit.

1315
00:53:25,506 --> 00:53:27,916
Why do you want to talk?

1316
00:53:28,916 --> 00:53:30,116
(Let's hear you out for now.)

1317
00:53:30,116 --> 00:53:32,446
You can't suddenly drag me somewhere or anything.

1318
00:53:32,956 --> 00:53:34,856
This isn't...

1319
00:53:34,856 --> 00:53:36,356
You said Fresh off the Sea wasn't like that.

1320
00:53:36,556 --> 00:53:37,956
No, but it changed.

1321
00:53:38,186 --> 00:53:39,596
(Feeling uneasy)

1322
00:53:40,226 --> 00:53:42,656
We went down the street, all excited.

1323
00:53:44,326 --> 00:53:47,336
(You Seok finally goes on a passionate rant.)

1324
00:53:48,136 --> 00:53:49,136
(Done briefing)

1325
00:53:49,136 --> 00:53:50,206
(Holding his breath)

1326
00:53:50,736 --> 00:53:53,236
The thing about the village hall elders was a lie,

1327
00:53:53,376 --> 00:53:54,506
- wasn't it?
- Yes.

1328
00:53:55,406 --> 00:53:56,906
They did head to the village hall.

1329
00:53:57,346 --> 00:53:58,846
Do you mean the village hall on Geoje Island?

1330
00:53:59,206 --> 00:54:00,746
(Laughing)

1331
00:54:00,746 --> 00:54:02,776
We never lied, you know?

1332
00:54:03,046 --> 00:54:04,686
You meant the village hall on Geoje Island?

1333
00:54:05,086 --> 00:54:06,886
Will they be receiving some onions,

1334
00:54:07,216 --> 00:54:08,316
not digging them up?

1335
00:54:09,216 --> 00:54:11,726
(Are they simply receiving some onions from the village hall?)

1336
00:54:12,686 --> 00:54:13,856
Is it to our left?

1337
00:54:14,026 --> 00:54:16,496
- Wherever it is, there must be...
- We must be here.

1338
00:54:16,826 --> 00:54:19,026
onions that need harvesting.

1339
00:54:21,636 --> 00:54:24,036
(Arrived)

1340
00:54:24,666 --> 00:54:26,066
Thank you, sir.

1341
00:54:26,936 --> 00:54:28,736
See you later, sir.

1342
00:54:29,576 --> 00:54:30,676
We're here.

1343
00:54:31,246 --> 00:54:33,916
- The elders are here.
- This town's village hall.

1344
00:54:33,976 --> 00:54:35,016
Right here.

1345
00:54:35,946 --> 00:54:37,416
(Songjin Village Village Hall, Senior Citizen Center)

1346
00:54:37,416 --> 00:54:39,386
(A village hall on Geoje Island)

1347
00:54:39,386 --> 00:54:40,486
(Songjin Village Village Hall, Senior Citizen Center)

1348
00:54:40,486 --> 00:54:42,656
(Onion greets the siblings.)

1349
00:54:42,886 --> 00:54:45,256
- This is where we get the onions.
- We get the onions here.

1350
00:54:45,256 --> 00:54:47,026
- Really? Not the field?
- From the elders.

1351
00:54:47,026 --> 00:54:49,456
- The village hall is right here.
- Are we not going to the field?

1352
00:54:49,456 --> 00:54:50,826
- We're not.
- What a bummer.

1353
00:54:51,296 --> 00:54:52,696
Do we go in together?

1354
00:54:52,696 --> 00:54:54,366
- Hi.
- Welcome.

1355
00:54:54,696 --> 00:54:56,766
Hi.

1356
00:54:56,766 --> 00:54:58,706
Hi.

1357
00:55:00,036 --> 00:55:01,036
(Geoje Island, Jangmok-myeon)

1358
00:55:01,036 --> 00:55:02,706
(10 minutes later)

1359
00:55:04,276 --> 00:55:05,746
(Working clothes)

1360
00:55:05,746 --> 00:55:06,816
I hate you.

1361
00:55:06,946 --> 00:55:08,276
(No surprises.)

1362
00:55:08,276 --> 00:55:09,576
- Was it not...
- I feel betrayed.

1363
00:55:09,576 --> 00:55:11,946
Don't film us. Don't, okay?

1364
00:55:13,256 --> 00:55:14,756
Such a move is...

1365
00:55:15,356 --> 00:55:17,626
- Fresh off the Sea...
- She's walking to the camera.

1366
00:55:17,626 --> 00:55:19,656
- Don't film us.
- Please wait.

1367
00:55:20,296 --> 00:55:21,596
Don't film us.

1368
00:55:21,596 --> 00:55:22,626
Stop.

1369
00:55:22,626 --> 00:55:24,566
Directors have the cameras.

1370
00:55:24,566 --> 00:55:27,036
Why is a producer filming with a phone?

1371
00:55:27,496 --> 00:55:29,136
Don't film us.

1372
00:55:29,706 --> 00:55:32,106
(Laughing)

1373
00:55:33,736 --> 00:55:35,736
First, warm up.

1374
00:55:35,736 --> 00:55:36,946
(Hae Joon warms up immediately.)

1375
00:55:36,946 --> 00:55:38,706
And then dig up onions.

1376
00:55:39,976 --> 00:55:41,546
If you're all warmed up...

1377
00:55:41,816 --> 00:55:42,876
What?

1378
00:55:43,616 --> 00:55:46,186
Where is it? Where do we go?

1379
00:55:46,186 --> 00:55:47,556
That way, and then this way.

1380
00:55:47,556 --> 00:55:48,756
Let's go, guys!

1381
00:55:51,256 --> 00:55:53,156
- Let's go.
- I'm going for...

1382
00:55:53,156 --> 00:55:54,656
(What's waiting for the onion expedition?)

1383
00:55:54,656 --> 00:55:56,696
a bedwetter style.

1384
00:55:56,766 --> 00:55:58,466
- Good.
- You get it, right?

1385
00:56:00,036 --> 00:56:02,766
Really, this place is amazing.

1386
00:56:04,866 --> 00:56:08,176
(This place boasts a lush, green view.)

1387
00:56:09,006 --> 00:56:12,876
(Songjin Village on the pristine coast of Geoje)

1388
00:56:13,446 --> 00:56:17,646
(Known as the Onion Village)

1389
00:56:17,846 --> 00:56:21,516
Thirty to forty percent is onions.

1390
00:56:21,516 --> 00:56:22,716
(Most of the village is onion fields.)

1391
00:56:23,226 --> 00:56:25,756
(Grown in the sea breeze blowing in from the nearby ocean)

1392
00:56:26,186 --> 00:56:29,556
These onions are sweeter and less pungent...

1393
00:56:29,726 --> 00:56:32,396
than other onions because they grow...

1394
00:56:32,426 --> 00:56:33,736
in the sea breeze.

1395
00:56:35,596 --> 00:56:37,736
(It's sweet even when eaten raw.)

1396
00:56:38,736 --> 00:56:41,736
(The onions are nice and plump.)

1397
00:56:41,736 --> 00:56:43,876
Please eat plenty and stay healthy.

1398
00:56:43,876 --> 00:56:46,346
(Geoje onion)

1399
00:56:47,076 --> 00:56:50,446
(Songjin Village, filled with onions everywhere)

1400
00:56:51,946 --> 00:56:54,216
This road is cute.

1401
00:56:54,216 --> 00:56:55,256
(Planning to work hard)

1402
00:56:55,916 --> 00:56:58,626
- Those are all onions.
- I thought they were green onions.

1403
00:56:58,626 --> 00:56:59,796
No, they're onions.

1404
00:56:59,796 --> 00:57:00,856
- Is this how they grow?
- Yes.

1405
00:57:00,856 --> 00:57:02,996
Green onions... So these are onions.

1406
00:57:03,766 --> 00:57:05,626
They look identical to green onions.

1407
00:57:05,626 --> 00:57:07,796
(At first glance, the onions look like wilted green onions.)

1408
00:57:08,136 --> 00:57:10,536
(But the onion stalks originally stand upright.)

1409
00:57:11,466 --> 00:57:14,106
(As nutrients gather in the onion bulb,)

1410
00:57:15,276 --> 00:57:17,776
(the stalks begin to fall over.)

1411
00:57:17,776 --> 00:57:20,046
(I got all the nutrients!)

1412
00:57:20,416 --> 00:57:23,246
(Just like rice bows its head as it ripens,)

1413
00:57:23,786 --> 00:57:27,786
(onions do the same.)

1414
00:57:29,316 --> 00:57:31,356
- Hi.
- Hi.

1415
00:57:31,686 --> 00:57:33,856
- Hi.
- Hi.

1416
00:57:34,456 --> 00:57:36,826
Where you are right now is...

1417
00:57:36,826 --> 00:57:39,596
- Songjinpo, the Onion Village.
- I see.

1418
00:57:39,696 --> 00:57:43,436
Carefully harvest the onions you need...

1419
00:57:44,436 --> 00:57:45,636
for the curry showdown.

1420
00:57:45,636 --> 00:57:46,706
(But to make the curry...)

1421
00:57:46,976 --> 00:57:48,936
Two onions?

1422
00:57:49,006 --> 00:57:50,846
- Are we getting paid?
- Are we getting paid?

1423
00:57:51,706 --> 00:57:52,906
But...

1424
00:57:53,146 --> 00:57:54,946
Our pay...

1425
00:57:54,946 --> 00:57:58,886
We'd like to know the reward we get before getting started.

1426
00:57:59,116 --> 00:58:00,686
We have to set it in stone right now.

1427
00:58:00,686 --> 00:58:01,756
Or they could change their words.

1428
00:58:01,756 --> 00:58:04,856
We'll tell you how much you have to harvest...

1429
00:58:04,856 --> 00:58:06,856
after you learn how to dig up the onions.

1430
00:58:07,356 --> 00:58:10,226
- Let's get to work.
- Okay.

1431
00:58:11,096 --> 00:58:12,326
We're in your care.

1432
00:58:12,996 --> 00:58:14,536
Likewise.

1433
00:58:14,536 --> 00:58:15,996
Let's start from here.

1434
00:58:16,266 --> 00:58:17,436
- Here?
- Yes.

1435
00:58:17,436 --> 00:58:18,466
Okay.

1436
00:58:18,506 --> 00:58:20,676
Pull one out at a time.

1437
00:58:20,676 --> 00:58:21,876
- One?
- Yes.

1438
00:58:22,006 --> 00:58:25,206
There's no need to rush. Take your time.

1439
00:58:25,206 --> 00:58:26,306
- Take our time?
- Take our time?

1440
00:58:27,146 --> 00:58:29,516
(Peel back the plastic covering.)

1441
00:58:29,516 --> 00:58:33,116
(Gently dig away the soil around the roots.)

1442
00:58:33,116 --> 00:58:36,656
(Lift it carefully without damaging the stalks or roots.)

1443
00:58:37,386 --> 00:58:38,886
(Focused and fascinated)

1444
00:58:38,886 --> 00:58:41,856
If the onion neck snaps off, it loses its market value.

1445
00:58:42,026 --> 00:58:43,796
The onion neck?

1446
00:58:44,326 --> 00:58:46,966
As you pull the onion out, you'll realise...

1447
00:58:46,966 --> 00:58:47,966
- how fragile the neck is.
- Right.

1448
00:58:48,436 --> 00:58:50,866
There are times when the neck will snap off.

1449
00:58:50,866 --> 00:58:54,236
It'll be bad if you damage the product.

1450
00:58:55,006 --> 00:58:56,706
- Don't damage the product.
- No.

1451
00:58:56,706 --> 00:58:58,106
- Got it.
- Damage...

1452
00:58:58,746 --> 00:59:00,316
- Give it a shot.
- Okay.

1453
00:59:01,346 --> 00:59:02,546
Even if you're only harvesting one,

1454
00:59:02,546 --> 00:59:04,246
- do it properly.
- Slowly?

1455
00:59:04,246 --> 00:59:05,986
You need to cut it later.

1456
00:59:05,986 --> 00:59:07,156
Cut what?

1457
00:59:07,456 --> 00:59:10,356
Cut it while leaving about 1cm of the neck intact.

1458
00:59:10,486 --> 00:59:12,856
Only then will the product still have its value.

1459
00:59:12,856 --> 00:59:13,856
I see.

1460
00:59:13,856 --> 00:59:15,126
(It can be shipped directly without any damage!)

1461
00:59:15,326 --> 00:59:17,026
- Got it?
- Yes.

1462
00:59:17,826 --> 00:59:19,126
Leave about this much.

1463
00:59:19,596 --> 00:59:21,936
Each net has to be filled with 20kg of onions.

1464
00:59:22,966 --> 00:59:25,806
(Gather the onions with the necks cut off.)

1465
00:59:26,136 --> 00:59:29,136
(Put them in an onion net.)

1466
00:59:29,506 --> 00:59:33,576
No other onions are as good as Geoje Island onions.

1467
00:59:33,976 --> 00:59:35,716
- Is that so?
- Geoje Island onions?

1468
00:59:35,716 --> 00:59:37,216
So they're the best.

1469
00:59:37,516 --> 00:59:39,146
- Give it a try.
- Okay.

1470
00:59:39,146 --> 00:59:40,656
- We'll start harvesting.
- Good.

1471
00:59:40,816 --> 00:59:42,316
Got it. Harvest nonstop.

1472
00:59:42,316 --> 00:59:43,426
- From now on.
- Nonstop.

1473
00:59:43,426 --> 00:59:44,526
Slowly.

1474
00:59:44,526 --> 00:59:45,526
Right.

1475
00:59:45,656 --> 00:59:47,926
- Shall we start?
- Use your fingers.

1476
00:59:48,556 --> 00:59:51,796
(Hoe at the ready.)

1477
00:59:52,226 --> 00:59:55,166
- Tear the plastic off ruthlessly.
- Tear it off ruthlessly.

1478
00:59:55,166 --> 00:59:56,166
Okay.

1479
00:59:56,166 --> 00:59:58,666
(Pulling out the onion)

1480
00:59:58,866 --> 01:00:01,236
(Pulling out the onion)

1481
01:00:01,876 --> 01:00:04,646
(Pulling out the onions is fun.)

1482
01:00:04,646 --> 01:00:06,476
Like this.

1483
01:00:06,476 --> 01:00:07,616
(Even the youngest, Yoon Seo)

1484
01:00:07,616 --> 01:00:09,016
Just pull it out.

1485
01:00:10,316 --> 01:00:12,646
- Okay.
- Don't damage it.

1486
01:00:12,646 --> 01:00:13,886
Okay, I'll be careful.

1487
01:00:14,816 --> 01:00:16,086
Be gentle.

1488
01:00:16,086 --> 01:00:17,426
(Being careful so as not to damage the onions)

1489
01:00:17,426 --> 01:00:18,456
Like so.

1490
01:00:18,456 --> 01:00:20,626
(Being careful so as not to damage the onions)

1491
01:00:21,026 --> 01:00:22,826
I think using my hands is better.

1492
01:00:22,826 --> 01:00:24,166
(Meanwhile, Sun Young is...)

1493
01:00:24,266 --> 01:00:25,296
Like this.

1494
01:00:26,396 --> 01:00:28,166
Like this. See?

1495
01:00:29,036 --> 01:00:30,836
The hoe is a hassle to me.

1496
01:00:30,836 --> 01:00:32,606
I know I won't break the neck.

1497
01:00:33,576 --> 01:00:35,436
Gently like so.

1498
01:00:35,806 --> 01:00:37,606
Wiggle it a little, and done.

1499
01:00:37,606 --> 01:00:39,006
Okay. Nice.

1500
01:00:39,706 --> 01:00:41,376
Like this.

1501
01:00:43,316 --> 01:00:45,016
All good, right? Nice.

1502
01:00:45,786 --> 01:00:48,356
(Working hard in their own ways)

1503
01:00:49,256 --> 01:00:50,786
I've never seen such huge onions.

1504
01:00:50,786 --> 01:00:52,256
(But harvesting onions...)

1505
01:00:52,826 --> 01:00:53,926
My eyes.

1506
01:00:53,926 --> 01:00:56,156
(can be hard on the eyes.)

1507
01:00:56,666 --> 01:00:59,296
(Blinking hard)

1508
01:01:01,896 --> 01:01:05,566
(Putting on sunglasses)

1509
01:01:05,566 --> 01:01:07,576
Sir, the hoe.

1510
01:01:12,476 --> 01:01:13,546
It's good.

1511
01:01:14,576 --> 01:01:17,346
(He bends down and starts working in earnest.)

1512
01:01:17,716 --> 01:01:22,116
(Wearing the item had a huge effect!)

1513
01:01:23,286 --> 01:01:25,686
Don't your eyes sting? You're wearing sunglasses.

1514
01:01:26,386 --> 01:01:27,496
You have an item.

1515
01:01:29,026 --> 01:01:30,066
Goodness.

1516
01:01:30,066 --> 01:01:32,996
(Yoon Seo, life is all about having the right items.)

1517
01:01:33,936 --> 01:01:36,096
(Onions piled up thanks to the item)

1518
01:01:37,166 --> 01:01:38,266
It's so big.

1519
01:01:38,266 --> 01:01:39,336
(Everyone is working hard.)

1520
01:01:39,536 --> 01:01:41,336
Do you see the onion bag in front of you?

1521
01:01:41,336 --> 01:01:42,476
Yes, go on.

1522
01:01:42,476 --> 01:01:45,546
One bag can fit 21kg of onions.

1523
01:01:45,546 --> 01:01:47,646
- Yes.
- We'll just fill up five bags...

1524
01:01:47,646 --> 01:01:49,476
and call it a day.

1525
01:01:49,476 --> 01:01:51,986
Five bags, so that's 105kg.

1526
01:01:51,986 --> 01:01:53,316
(5 bags equal 105kg maximum.)

1527
01:01:53,316 --> 01:01:55,586
How many onions fit in one bag?

1528
01:01:56,356 --> 01:01:58,386
- About 50 to 60.
- About 50 to 60.

1529
01:01:58,386 --> 01:02:00,196
About 50 to 60 onions in one bag.

1530
01:02:00,196 --> 01:02:03,826
That means, 5 by 50 equals 250. Okay.

1531
01:02:04,926 --> 01:02:07,536
- We need 250.
- We can finish it in no time.

1532
01:02:07,536 --> 01:02:08,566
Let's go.

1533
01:02:10,206 --> 01:02:12,336
I'll be fast. I'll really dig up a lot.

1534
01:02:13,906 --> 01:02:15,076
I got it.

1535
01:02:15,806 --> 01:02:17,676
Be fast.

1536
01:02:17,676 --> 01:02:20,816
(Wasp Sun Young mode)

1537
01:02:21,576 --> 01:02:23,816
- Am I going fast enough?
- You're good.

1538
01:02:23,816 --> 01:02:25,186
Thanks. I can do well.

1539
01:02:25,786 --> 01:02:27,416
I'm a countryside girl.

1540
01:02:28,486 --> 01:02:29,556
Okay.

1541
01:02:29,956 --> 01:02:31,226
I got it.

1542
01:02:32,156 --> 01:02:34,556
(Going berserk)

1543
01:02:35,456 --> 01:02:36,526
It's no joke, right?

1544
01:02:37,696 --> 01:02:39,066
I'm getting faster.

1545
01:02:41,266 --> 01:02:44,166
(I'll follow you, Sun Young.)

1546
01:02:45,236 --> 01:02:46,266
Two hundred fifty.

1547
01:02:46,266 --> 01:02:49,536
(Do not forget 250.)

1548
01:02:51,146 --> 01:02:54,046
(Quietly focusing on his labour to hit the goal)

1549
01:02:54,246 --> 01:02:55,876
This isn't much.

1550
01:02:56,216 --> 01:02:57,946
- Exactly. It isn't that much.
- We'll be done in no time.

1551
01:02:58,716 --> 01:03:01,116
Take up farming after you retire. You're good.

1552
01:03:01,656 --> 01:03:02,686
You can take up farming.

1553
01:03:02,886 --> 01:03:05,526
This is the third time someone has suggested that. Right?

1554
01:03:05,526 --> 01:03:06,556
(Following the sea squirt and seaweed labours,)

1555
01:03:06,826 --> 01:03:07,826
They're casting us.

1556
01:03:07,826 --> 01:03:09,656
(the onion labour adds to the consecutive casting offers.)

1557
01:03:09,826 --> 01:03:12,126
- We're lucky.
- Sorry?

1558
01:03:12,226 --> 01:03:14,236
We're lucky to meet the actors we like.

1559
01:03:14,236 --> 01:03:17,106
Right. You know Gwan Sik, right?

1560
01:03:17,106 --> 01:03:18,936
Yes, we know. We know all of you.

1561
01:03:19,436 --> 01:03:21,976
(Today, he's Farmer Gwan Sik.)

1562
01:03:22,206 --> 01:03:23,646
And you are Sun Woo's mom.

1563
01:03:24,906 --> 01:03:26,276
Where's that, Ssangmun-dong.

1564
01:03:26,976 --> 01:03:28,346
Ssangmun-dong.

1565
01:03:29,516 --> 01:03:31,646
You all look much prettier in person.

1566
01:03:31,646 --> 01:03:33,656
- Thank you.
- My gosh.

1567
01:03:35,156 --> 01:03:36,156
You're driving me crazy.

1568
01:03:36,156 --> 01:03:38,386
(Yoon Seo, who's much prettier in person as verified by the lady)

1569
01:03:38,956 --> 01:03:41,056
(Cheered by the compliment, they work hard again.)

1570
01:03:41,256 --> 01:03:44,366
(Hoe ready)

1571
01:03:44,666 --> 01:03:47,466
(Using her hand again)

1572
01:03:47,766 --> 01:03:49,766
I need to use a hoe for this.

1573
01:03:52,966 --> 01:03:55,206
This is a really tough one.

1574
01:03:56,036 --> 01:03:57,046
This is huge.

1575
01:03:57,046 --> 01:03:58,576
(A tough one halts Wasp Sun Young.)

1576
01:03:59,376 --> 01:04:03,416
(She fixes her posture.)

1577
01:04:03,916 --> 01:04:06,346
(The fight against the tough one starts.)

1578
01:04:06,686 --> 01:04:08,716
(She grabs the onion stalk like she's grabbing hair.)

1579
01:04:09,316 --> 01:04:10,356
Let's go.

1580
01:04:11,256 --> 01:04:12,756
Okay, don't pull here.

1581
01:04:13,626 --> 01:04:14,626
Let's go.

1582
01:04:15,956 --> 01:04:17,126
Curry...

1583
01:04:17,126 --> 01:04:18,526
(Gently digging up the onion stuck in the ground)

1584
01:04:18,826 --> 01:04:21,336
(Pull it out gently so it doesn't bruise.)

1585
01:04:21,336 --> 01:04:22,636
Done. It's no joke.

1586
01:04:23,096 --> 01:04:25,706
Okay, the big ones.

1587
01:04:26,606 --> 01:04:29,076
Nice. It's huge.

1588
01:04:29,076 --> 01:04:31,676
(She keeps pulling out big ones.)

1589
01:04:32,546 --> 01:04:34,076
- Gosh, it's big.
- My gosh.

1590
01:04:35,016 --> 01:04:36,016
Look at this.

1591
01:04:36,016 --> 01:04:37,046
(Hae Joon finds a big one too.)

1592
01:04:39,216 --> 01:04:41,686
This is an onion? Watermelon...

1593
01:04:41,686 --> 01:04:43,416
(It should be called a watermelon.)

1594
01:04:44,126 --> 01:04:45,326
This is huge.

1595
01:04:47,996 --> 01:04:50,196
(Struggling)

1596
01:04:51,226 --> 01:04:53,166
(The onion is as big as Yoon Seo's face.)

1597
01:04:54,166 --> 01:04:56,866
- I feel good when I find big ones.
- Me too.

1598
01:04:57,666 --> 01:05:00,576
Do the big ones and small ones taste the same?

1599
01:05:01,376 --> 01:05:02,576
The big ones taste good.

1600
01:05:02,576 --> 01:05:04,006
- The big ones taste good?
- Yes.

1601
01:05:04,406 --> 01:05:07,416
- The big ones are juicy.
- Juicy.

1602
01:05:07,716 --> 01:05:09,616
- And it has more sugar.
- It has more sugar.

1603
01:05:09,876 --> 01:05:12,246
I should buy big ones when I go grocery shopping.

1604
01:05:13,316 --> 01:05:14,656
This is super huge.

1605
01:05:16,216 --> 01:05:18,786
(It's huge, so it's tasty.)

1606
01:05:18,786 --> 01:05:19,856
It's huge.

1607
01:05:20,156 --> 01:05:22,156
This is no joke. Okay.

1608
01:05:22,756 --> 01:05:23,966
Nice, the big ones.

1609
01:05:24,666 --> 01:05:26,936
This is big.

1610
01:05:27,466 --> 01:05:28,496
Dear viewers.

1611
01:05:29,396 --> 01:05:32,406
Geoje Island's onions...

1612
01:05:32,836 --> 01:05:35,836
grow in the soils incomparable to others,

1613
01:05:35,906 --> 01:05:38,376
and they're juicy because of the sea breeze.

1614
01:05:39,906 --> 01:05:40,916
(Pfft)

1615
01:05:41,976 --> 01:05:44,116
- Okay.
- Soil gets in your mouth, right?

1616
01:05:44,116 --> 01:05:45,186
Yes.

1617
01:05:45,586 --> 01:05:46,956
Everyone should wear a mask.

1618
01:05:46,956 --> 01:05:48,816
(It's harder to work because the wind blows the soil.)

1619
01:05:49,356 --> 01:05:52,426
This soil is nothing if I can please the viewers.

1620
01:05:53,486 --> 01:05:55,926
Hello. I came to harvest onions.

1621
01:05:55,926 --> 01:05:56,996
(The rising communicator)

1622
01:05:56,996 --> 01:05:58,066
This is a small one.

1623
01:05:58,926 --> 01:05:59,996
This is how you harvest it.

1624
01:06:01,436 --> 01:06:02,536
Do this with the hoe.

1625
01:06:02,536 --> 01:06:03,866
(The rising communicator stumbles, but she still does her best.)

1626
01:06:05,406 --> 01:06:06,466
You can pull it out.

1627
01:06:06,866 --> 01:06:07,936
Bye.

1628
01:06:07,936 --> 01:06:09,476
(She doesn't have much to say.)

1629
01:06:10,036 --> 01:06:12,746
Is it your first time working on a field with so many onions?

1630
01:06:12,846 --> 01:06:14,576
- Yes.
- Definitely.

1631
01:06:14,976 --> 01:06:17,716
Do you work from 7am to 5pm?

1632
01:06:17,716 --> 01:06:19,746
- From 7am to 5pm?
- Yes.

1633
01:06:19,746 --> 01:06:20,756
(During the harvest season, they work all day.)

1634
01:06:20,756 --> 01:06:21,986
This is tough work.

1635
01:06:21,986 --> 01:06:24,186
Exactly. How do you do this? I admire you.

1636
01:06:24,186 --> 01:06:25,586
(Experiencing it herself makes her admire them.)

1637
01:06:25,586 --> 01:06:28,296
How can you do this work for so long?

1638
01:06:31,066 --> 01:06:32,066
That's so big.

1639
01:06:32,596 --> 01:06:35,266
(Meanwhile, Hae Joon digs up onions silently.)

1640
01:06:35,766 --> 01:06:36,796
(Grunting)

1641
01:06:37,006 --> 01:06:39,766
(The only sounds he makes are his grunts.)

1642
01:06:40,906 --> 01:06:41,936
(Sighing)

1643
01:06:43,706 --> 01:06:45,446
- Gosh, my back.
- Your back hurts, right?

1644
01:06:46,046 --> 01:06:47,116
Goodness.

1645
01:06:47,576 --> 01:06:49,016
Let's go.

1646
01:06:49,876 --> 01:06:52,746
(Tall Hae Joon has to bend more, so it's hard for him.)

1647
01:06:53,416 --> 01:06:55,456
(Nevertheless, he has to do it.)

1648
01:06:56,156 --> 01:06:58,686
- You can't be silent, Hae Joon.
- What?

1649
01:06:59,226 --> 01:07:01,526
- You can't only work. Speak.
- Work?

1650
01:07:02,456 --> 01:07:05,266
Tell us an onion-related story.

1651
01:07:07,736 --> 01:07:10,666
(He's a fool who has his eyes only on onions.)

1652
01:07:11,336 --> 01:07:13,936
This job is unexpectedly tough.

1653
01:07:13,936 --> 01:07:15,006
(They've been crouching to work for 50 minutes.)

1654
01:07:15,536 --> 01:07:16,606
My back.

1655
01:07:16,606 --> 01:07:17,846
(The crouching position makes her back ache.)

1656
01:07:18,306 --> 01:07:20,476
(They're getting exhausted.)

1657
01:07:20,816 --> 01:07:21,846
Dear viewers.

1658
01:07:22,846 --> 01:07:24,446
I'm so exhausted.

1659
01:07:25,246 --> 01:07:27,516
(The communication king even relays the news of her being exhausted.)

1660
01:07:28,056 --> 01:07:29,756
This is very tough.

1661
01:07:29,756 --> 01:07:30,756
(The exhaustion reminds them of a song.)

1662
01:07:30,856 --> 01:07:33,756
(Yangpa sings "Love.. What is it")

1663
01:07:33,956 --> 01:07:40,026
(Please tell me I've heard wrong)

1664
01:07:40,326 --> 01:07:45,466
(Onion, what are you to make me cry)

1665
01:07:45,736 --> 01:07:48,676
(Why do you drive me crazy)

1666
01:07:49,006 --> 01:07:53,746
(I hate myself more)

1667
01:07:53,746 --> 01:07:57,086
(For still loving you)

1668
01:07:57,616 --> 01:08:01,386
(Onions, I love you.)

1669
01:08:01,956 --> 01:08:03,256
Didn't we dig up around 100?

1670
01:08:04,086 --> 01:08:05,156
- One hundred?
- Yes.

1671
01:08:05,156 --> 01:08:06,826
- Really?
- Did we not?

1672
01:08:07,526 --> 01:08:09,526
(Still a long way to go until the target amount)

1673
01:08:10,196 --> 01:08:13,296
I'll make sure we dig up 250.

1674
01:08:13,666 --> 01:08:15,766
When will we harvest 250? Can we do it in no time?

1675
01:08:17,506 --> 01:08:19,966
We didn't know how important the lots were.

1676
01:08:20,676 --> 01:08:22,736
(Suddenly feeling wronged)

1677
01:08:25,576 --> 01:08:28,376
You Seok is resting at home because he got lucky...

1678
01:08:28,976 --> 01:08:30,046
with the draw?

1679
01:08:30,986 --> 01:08:32,886
What are You Seok and Jung Ah doing?

1680
01:08:32,886 --> 01:08:34,916
Make You Seok do some hard labour too!

1681
01:08:35,686 --> 01:08:38,956
How is this possible? This labour is no joke!

1682
01:08:38,956 --> 01:08:39,956
(While Sun Young gradually gets angrier,)

1683
01:08:40,386 --> 01:08:43,526
(back at home...)

1684
01:08:43,526 --> 01:08:44,596
I'm bored.

1685
01:08:46,496 --> 01:08:48,996
(She's leisurely taking care of herself with the music.)

1686
01:08:49,896 --> 01:08:52,466
(Shaking)

1687
01:08:52,906 --> 01:08:54,806
It's boring without the rest.

1688
01:08:56,236 --> 01:08:59,346
(Her dance betrays her words.)

1689
01:09:00,046 --> 01:09:02,816
(Where is You Seok?)

1690
01:09:05,186 --> 01:09:09,856
(You Seok is reading a book.)

1691
01:09:11,126 --> 01:09:14,696
(He looks like he's enjoying the gap between classes.)

1692
01:09:16,026 --> 01:09:19,296
(Senior Kang, acting major)

1693
01:09:20,766 --> 01:09:24,966
(Senior Kang is focused on his book.)

1694
01:09:26,736 --> 01:09:29,336
(Senior Kang reads the same page of the same book every day.)

1695
01:09:29,976 --> 01:09:31,946
(Still on the same page)

1696
01:09:32,346 --> 01:09:34,316
(Blinking)

1697
01:09:34,316 --> 01:09:36,946
(But his eyes look very bored.)

1698
01:09:38,746 --> 01:09:41,486
(He loses his focus in less than a page.)

1699
01:09:41,686 --> 01:09:43,656
(Distracted)

1700
01:09:44,386 --> 01:09:46,456
(Sunken eyes)

1701
01:09:46,456 --> 01:09:48,196
(He's just bored.)

1702
01:09:49,096 --> 01:09:50,996
You Seok, did we finish the snacks yesterday?

1703
01:09:50,996 --> 01:09:53,026
(Senior Kang closes his book as soon as Jung Ah calls.)

1704
01:09:53,026 --> 01:09:54,036
We finished them, right?

1705
01:09:55,366 --> 01:09:56,436
Seriously.

1706
01:09:56,436 --> 01:09:57,606
(Chewing)

1707
01:09:58,206 --> 01:09:59,906
I don't know if I should be doing this.

1708
01:10:00,876 --> 01:10:02,836
- I feel bad.
- I feel so bad for feeling bored.

1709
01:10:03,946 --> 01:10:07,316
(Enjoying the free time with the snack)

1710
01:10:08,216 --> 01:10:10,276
Let us take pictures.

1711
01:10:10,686 --> 01:10:12,386
The others must be sweating and working hard.

1712
01:10:13,546 --> 01:10:16,956
(They do them.)

1713
01:10:17,356 --> 01:10:18,426
(We'll leave memories.)

1714
01:10:18,426 --> 01:10:19,686
One, two, three.

1715
01:10:21,926 --> 01:10:25,096
(A sweet photo of them)

1716
01:10:25,596 --> 01:10:27,936
We do everything, from riding a boat to working on a farm.

1717
01:10:27,936 --> 01:10:29,466
- Seriously.
- Yes. You've been through a lot.

1718
01:10:29,936 --> 01:10:31,006
We're almost done.

1719
01:10:31,006 --> 01:10:32,136
(They're almost done with harvesting onions.)

1720
01:10:33,606 --> 01:10:34,736
I'm good at this.

1721
01:10:36,736 --> 01:10:39,306
(Everyone becomes an expert in onion harvest.)

1722
01:10:39,746 --> 01:10:42,076
(She sacrifices her knees.)

1723
01:10:42,476 --> 01:10:43,476
There you go.

1724
01:10:43,476 --> 01:10:45,286
(Working hard until the end)

1725
01:10:45,816 --> 01:10:49,956
(They don't stop until they hit the 250 mark.)

1726
01:10:51,186 --> 01:10:53,486
(Surrounded by onions)

1727
01:10:54,226 --> 01:10:56,326
- I think we're done.
- Really?

1728
01:10:56,326 --> 01:10:57,396
We have enough.

1729
01:10:57,766 --> 01:10:58,796
This is enough.

1730
01:10:59,866 --> 01:11:02,736
One can cut off the stalks, and one can bag the onions.

1731
01:11:03,466 --> 01:11:05,566
Do I cut off this much?

1732
01:11:05,566 --> 01:11:06,636
(How do you cut off onion stalks?)

1733
01:11:06,636 --> 01:11:08,836
- You can hold the leaf.
- Yes.

1734
01:11:09,336 --> 01:11:10,336
This much.

1735
01:11:10,976 --> 01:11:13,576
As it dries, it shrinks.

1736
01:11:13,576 --> 01:11:15,746
So leave quite a bit.

1737
01:11:15,746 --> 01:11:16,946
- Leave a bit?
- About two centimetres?

1738
01:11:16,946 --> 01:11:18,016
Okay.

1739
01:11:18,716 --> 01:11:20,956
(With scissors)

1740
01:11:20,956 --> 01:11:23,756
(He starts cutting onion stalks.)

1741
01:11:24,356 --> 01:11:27,126
(Humming)

1742
01:11:27,826 --> 01:11:30,026
(He still doesn't talk, but he pumps himself up by humming.)

1743
01:11:31,566 --> 01:11:34,366
The scissors are convenient.

1744
01:11:34,666 --> 01:11:36,366
This is nothing compared to digging them up.

1745
01:11:37,066 --> 01:11:39,136
I love onions.

1746
01:11:39,136 --> 01:11:41,206
I add onions to almost all the food.

1747
01:11:41,206 --> 01:11:42,206
(A cutting machine with nonstop communication activates.)

1748
01:11:44,106 --> 01:11:46,876
(The art student's bold cuts)

1749
01:11:47,476 --> 01:11:48,976
I think I'm good.

1750
01:11:49,946 --> 01:11:51,046
I'll be fast.

1751
01:11:52,946 --> 01:11:54,816
You should do it right even if it slows you down.

1752
01:11:56,516 --> 01:11:59,156
(Very meticulous)

1753
01:11:59,356 --> 01:12:01,626
He's become like the owner.

1754
01:12:03,426 --> 01:12:06,566
(Owner Hae Joon cuts the onion stalks properly.)

1755
01:12:06,966 --> 01:12:09,336
(He gathers the stalk-less onions.)

1756
01:12:09,566 --> 01:12:11,436
- Put them inside.
- Pardon?

1757
01:12:11,436 --> 01:12:13,136
- Into the bag.
- Into the bag?

1758
01:12:13,136 --> 01:12:15,706
(Time to put them into the bag)

1759
01:12:16,136 --> 01:12:17,446
I can't just pour them in, can I?

1760
01:12:18,276 --> 01:12:19,776
- Just pour them.
- I can?

1761
01:12:20,946 --> 01:12:22,876
(Embarrassed)

1762
01:12:23,416 --> 01:12:25,586
(My precious onions)

1763
01:12:26,116 --> 01:12:29,356
(Rolling into the bag)

1764
01:12:29,686 --> 01:12:32,526
(The bag is more than half full.)

1765
01:12:32,826 --> 01:12:33,926
It's heavy.

1766
01:12:36,356 --> 01:12:38,996
(Yoon Seo has it completely filled.)

1767
01:12:38,996 --> 01:12:40,266
- Pour them out.
- Pour them out?

1768
01:12:40,266 --> 01:12:41,296
Yes.

1769
01:12:42,196 --> 01:12:43,196
Yes.

1770
01:12:43,196 --> 01:12:44,636
(Rolling down)

1771
01:12:44,636 --> 01:12:45,736
Done.

1772
01:12:45,736 --> 01:12:47,806
(They've already filled up almost 2 full bags of onions.)

1773
01:12:47,806 --> 01:12:49,006
I'll work hard.

1774
01:12:49,136 --> 01:12:50,746
Please give me a lot of wage.

1775
01:12:50,746 --> 01:12:51,746
(The onions Sun Young cut while chattering)

1776
01:12:51,746 --> 01:12:52,746
Let's go.

1777
01:12:52,746 --> 01:12:54,176
(Hae Joon lifts them up.)

1778
01:12:56,146 --> 01:12:58,286
(He's good at pouring them out now.)

1779
01:12:58,816 --> 01:13:01,186
(3 bags full already)

1780
01:13:01,356 --> 01:13:02,356
- Now...
- We're done.

1781
01:13:02,356 --> 01:13:03,656
(Feeling proud)

1782
01:13:04,016 --> 01:13:05,486
You need to add more.

1783
01:13:06,286 --> 01:13:07,926
- You need to add more.
- Fill it up completely?

1784
01:13:07,926 --> 01:13:09,026
Completely.

1785
01:13:09,226 --> 01:13:11,096
(This isn't easy.)

1786
01:13:11,726 --> 01:13:13,366
- You can put that in too.
- This is good, right?

1787
01:13:13,526 --> 01:13:14,696
Look at this.

1788
01:13:14,896 --> 01:13:16,636
- Put that in as well.
- My goodness.

1789
01:13:16,636 --> 01:13:17,636
(Another big one)

1790
01:13:18,006 --> 01:13:21,336
(He keeps adding the onions.)

1791
01:13:21,436 --> 01:13:23,506
It's completely full.

1792
01:13:23,506 --> 01:13:24,546
My gosh.

1793
01:13:25,146 --> 01:13:26,346
Put in a few more.

1794
01:13:27,246 --> 01:13:28,876
- Three or four.
- More?

1795
01:13:31,386 --> 01:13:32,416
One more.

1796
01:13:35,056 --> 01:13:36,356
- One more.
- One more?

1797
01:13:37,216 --> 01:13:39,326
(How many more are you adding?)

1798
01:13:39,326 --> 01:13:40,356
You need to fill it up completely.

1799
01:13:40,626 --> 01:13:42,996
(Even when you fill it up until it spills over,)

1800
01:13:42,996 --> 01:13:45,626
(it doesn't break.)

1801
01:13:45,926 --> 01:13:47,536
(1 completely-filled up bag)

1802
01:13:47,536 --> 01:13:49,666
It's done. Try carrying it.

1803
01:13:50,836 --> 01:13:51,836
(Surprised)

1804
01:13:54,606 --> 01:13:55,606
(Struggling)

1805
01:13:55,606 --> 01:13:56,706
Gosh, this...

1806
01:13:56,706 --> 01:13:58,406
(Heavy)

1807
01:13:59,206 --> 01:14:00,676
- Let's carry it.
- To that side?

1808
01:14:01,346 --> 01:14:02,446
This is heavy.

1809
01:14:02,446 --> 01:14:03,646
(An unexpected workout with the 20kg load)

1810
01:14:04,646 --> 01:14:05,686
My gosh.

1811
01:14:05,816 --> 01:14:08,256
(4 more bags to go)

1812
01:14:08,656 --> 01:14:11,256
(He delivers 1 more bag right away.)

1813
01:14:11,786 --> 01:14:13,356
Here they are. Which bag do I fill up?

1814
01:14:13,356 --> 01:14:14,396
(Down to one last precious onion)

1815
01:14:14,856 --> 01:14:17,866
We got a really big one.

1816
01:14:17,866 --> 01:14:18,996
(They keep filling up the bags.)

1817
01:14:19,226 --> 01:14:20,666
We're done.

1818
01:14:21,666 --> 01:14:23,696
(The 3rd bag)

1819
01:14:24,266 --> 01:14:26,506
- The onions are heavy.
- How did he carry it by himself?

1820
01:14:26,766 --> 01:14:29,806
I'm glad I did something on the show.

1821
01:14:31,006 --> 01:14:32,476
I agree.

1822
01:14:32,476 --> 01:14:34,146
Was it 20kg?

1823
01:14:34,146 --> 01:14:35,246
It's 20kg.

1824
01:14:35,246 --> 01:14:37,016
(They harvested 100kg in total.)

1825
01:14:37,816 --> 01:14:38,846
(And the present from the onion farm)

1826
01:14:38,846 --> 01:14:40,286
- You worked hard.
- Thanks.

1827
01:14:40,286 --> 01:14:41,286
Here.

1828
01:14:41,556 --> 01:14:42,816
Next.

1829
01:14:42,986 --> 01:14:44,586
- Thank you.
- Thank you, sir.

1830
01:14:44,586 --> 01:14:45,626
Thank you.

1831
01:14:45,926 --> 01:14:47,056
Thank you.

1832
01:14:47,356 --> 01:14:50,866
(The onions of Songjinpo, Geoje, harvested by the onion expedition,)

1833
01:14:51,366 --> 01:14:54,266
(were delivered to the customers...)

1834
01:14:56,036 --> 01:14:58,636
(around the nation.)

1835
01:14:59,006 --> 01:15:01,276
- Thank you. Bye.
- Thank you. Bye.

1836
01:15:01,276 --> 01:15:02,806
- Good job.
- Bye.

1837
01:15:02,806 --> 01:15:05,006
- Thank you.
- Bye.

1838
01:15:05,006 --> 01:15:07,046
- Good job.
- Thanks for the onions.

1839
01:15:07,046 --> 01:15:09,516
- Stay healthy.
- You guys are amazing, really.

1840
01:15:10,676 --> 01:15:12,846
- Hae Joon, aren't you exhausted?
- It's okay.

1841
01:15:13,046 --> 01:15:14,186
Goodness.

1842
01:15:14,186 --> 01:15:16,316
We can't be worn out just from working this much...

1843
01:15:16,316 --> 01:15:17,556
- when they work all day.
- Exactly.

1844
01:15:17,556 --> 01:15:18,826
What we did was nothing.

1845
01:15:18,826 --> 01:15:19,926
(They appreciate the value of labour once again.)

1846
01:15:19,926 --> 01:15:20,956
Seriously.

1847
01:15:22,026 --> 01:15:23,026
(Grunting)

1848
01:15:23,726 --> 01:15:24,726
(Grunting)

1849
01:15:25,066 --> 01:15:26,396
Gosh, my back.

1850
01:15:27,266 --> 01:15:29,296
(The onion expedition gets ready to go home with grunts.)

1851
01:15:29,296 --> 01:15:30,396
I'm beat.

1852
01:15:31,166 --> 01:15:32,506
(His hair is a mess.)

1853
01:15:32,506 --> 01:15:34,936
(The aftertaste of the labour leaves him dishevelled.)

1854
01:15:35,706 --> 01:15:38,846
(His neat self before the work is nowhere to be found.)

1855
01:15:39,546 --> 01:15:41,616
You don't know when you'll be taken away to work,

1856
01:15:41,616 --> 01:15:45,016
so I'll prepare all my clothes to be suitable for labour.

1857
01:15:46,146 --> 01:15:47,186
(Tattered)

1858
01:15:47,186 --> 01:15:48,856
(He still can't believe he was dragged to an onion farm.)

1859
01:15:49,486 --> 01:15:51,226
(After a long struggle, they head home with the onions...)

1860
01:15:51,226 --> 01:15:52,326
(for the curry showdown.)

1861
01:15:53,056 --> 01:15:55,496
(The front row passengers fall asleep from the hard labour.)

1862
01:15:55,756 --> 01:15:57,566
(But he's wide awake.)

1863
01:15:57,896 --> 01:16:00,296
(Rolling his eyes)

1864
01:16:01,796 --> 01:16:05,436
They've all fallen asleep.

1865
01:16:08,976 --> 01:16:10,006
Sorry?

1866
01:16:10,336 --> 01:16:12,506
I... Nothing.

1867
01:16:12,506 --> 01:16:13,706
She's not sleeping.

1868
01:16:17,546 --> 01:16:20,586
If they're sleeping...

1869
01:16:21,416 --> 01:16:23,356
Is he talking to himself?

1870
01:16:24,526 --> 01:16:25,856
I like being quiet.

1871
01:16:26,926 --> 01:16:28,696
I enjoy the times...

1872
01:16:29,096 --> 01:16:31,196
where you can understand each other...

1873
01:16:31,196 --> 01:16:32,196
without talking.

1874
01:16:32,196 --> 01:16:33,196
(Hae Joon starts a monologue.)

1875
01:16:33,496 --> 01:16:36,436
It must've weighed on his mind that he was quiet during work.

1876
01:16:36,436 --> 01:16:38,206
You didn't say anything...

1877
01:16:38,206 --> 01:16:39,836
when you were working.

1878
01:16:40,466 --> 01:16:42,276
When I was working on the onions,

1879
01:16:42,836 --> 01:16:45,046
I talked too little.

1880
01:16:45,046 --> 01:16:46,206
You talked way too little.

1881
01:16:46,206 --> 01:16:47,306
- I tried to get you to talk, - And...

1882
01:16:47,306 --> 01:16:48,376
but your answers...

1883
01:16:49,116 --> 01:16:51,946
- I want to continue...
- He's very funny.

1884
01:16:51,946 --> 01:16:53,716
to talk to myself.

1885
01:16:54,716 --> 01:16:57,456
I refuse to have a conversation.

1886
01:16:57,986 --> 01:16:59,886
He refuses to have a conversation and will talk to himself.

1887
01:16:59,886 --> 01:17:02,326
Why do you refuse to have a conversation?

1888
01:17:02,326 --> 01:17:03,326
(She finds him very funny.)

1889
01:17:03,326 --> 01:17:04,566
(Don't talk to me. I'll talk to myself.)

1890
01:17:04,566 --> 01:17:05,666
I...

1891
01:17:05,896 --> 01:17:07,196
When I dug up onions,

1892
01:17:08,066 --> 01:17:09,766
there was dust on my mouth,

1893
01:17:10,366 --> 01:17:12,066
and I felt thirsty.

1894
01:17:12,936 --> 01:17:15,006
I wanted to peel the onion and chew it.

1895
01:17:15,876 --> 01:17:16,876
I...

1896
01:17:17,606 --> 01:17:20,306
The big onion...

1897
01:17:21,216 --> 01:17:24,116
is very juicy, apparently.

1898
01:17:24,116 --> 01:17:27,216
I could've asked for water,

1899
01:17:27,286 --> 01:17:28,956
- Why didn't you?
- but my pride didn't allow it.

1900
01:17:30,256 --> 01:17:31,886
Why does that hurt your pride?

1901
01:17:32,186 --> 01:17:34,186
- For something so trivial...
- Trivial.

1902
01:17:34,186 --> 01:17:36,226
My thirst...

1903
01:17:36,226 --> 01:17:37,296
(His pride didn't allow him to feel thirsty.)

1904
01:17:37,896 --> 01:17:40,526
Is this a monologue or a conversation?

1905
01:17:40,866 --> 01:17:43,166
- I'm getting confused, but...
- But.

1906
01:17:43,236 --> 01:17:45,736
after today's filming ends,

1907
01:17:46,366 --> 01:17:48,106
you know the house?

1908
01:17:48,736 --> 01:17:51,346
In the house,

1909
01:17:51,946 --> 01:17:53,946
- I plan to do that.
- "That."

1910
01:17:54,246 --> 01:17:55,776
- Curry?
- It's...

1911
01:17:57,076 --> 01:17:58,486
"Not Fresh off the Sea."

1912
01:17:58,486 --> 01:18:00,286
"Not Fresh off the Sea."

1913
01:18:00,986 --> 01:18:02,016
How does it sound?

1914
01:18:02,256 --> 01:18:03,486
You'll steal the show?

1915
01:18:04,116 --> 01:18:06,756
- You'll take over?
- I don't like the title.

1916
01:18:06,756 --> 01:18:08,096
- You don't like the title.
- So...

1917
01:18:09,626 --> 01:18:10,996
I'll change Fresh off the Sea...

1918
01:18:11,726 --> 01:18:14,166
- to "Not Fresh off the Sea." - "Not Fresh off the Sea."

1919
01:18:15,196 --> 01:18:18,306
Look forward to "Not Fresh off the Sea."

1920
01:18:19,136 --> 01:18:21,936
(They return home with Hae Joon's never-ending monologue.)

1921
01:18:22,436 --> 01:18:24,976
Let's carry the onions

1922
01:18:26,476 --> 01:18:27,506
(The onion expedition is back home.)

1923
01:18:27,506 --> 01:18:28,546
Gosh, Hae Joon.

1924
01:18:31,286 --> 01:18:35,016
(Father.)

1925
01:18:36,586 --> 01:18:37,586
Take this.

1926
01:18:38,056 --> 01:18:39,056
Give me that.

1927
01:18:40,756 --> 01:18:42,696
It's heavy. Be careful.

1928
01:18:42,696 --> 01:18:44,326
(The onions are heavier because they're bigger and fuller.)

1929
01:18:44,866 --> 01:18:45,926
(Jung Ah goes to welcome the others.)

1930
01:18:45,926 --> 01:18:47,466
- You'll dirty your clothes.
- It's okay.

1931
01:18:47,466 --> 01:18:49,336
- Go on.
- Go, it's heavy.

1932
01:18:49,336 --> 01:18:50,396
- Let's go.
- Hurry up.

1933
01:18:51,066 --> 01:18:53,566
(They all head back to the Siblings' House.)

1934
01:18:54,576 --> 01:18:55,706
There are onions here!

1935
01:18:56,636 --> 01:18:59,046
- What? We have onions.
- There are onions here.

1936
01:18:59,046 --> 01:19:00,406
There are onions. Darn it.

1937
01:19:00,946 --> 01:19:02,176
They were right in front of the house.

1938
01:19:03,046 --> 01:19:04,486
We could've just pulled them out using bare hands.

1939
01:19:04,486 --> 01:19:05,946
My gosh.

1940
01:19:06,186 --> 01:19:07,546
But they're not comparable.

1941
01:19:07,546 --> 01:19:10,116
You can't compare them. When you pulled out the stalk...

1942
01:19:10,116 --> 01:19:12,186
- The stalk was so much thicker.
- That's some crazy onion.

1943
01:19:12,186 --> 01:19:14,356
- Isn't it huge?
- It's crazy.

1944
01:19:14,356 --> 01:19:16,996
The bigger it is, the juicier it is.

1945
01:19:16,996 --> 01:19:18,166
My gosh.

1946
01:19:18,266 --> 01:19:20,326
- You worked so hard.
- It's so...

1947
01:19:21,236 --> 01:19:24,806
(The last ingredient for the curry showdown has arrived.)

1948
01:19:24,806 --> 01:19:26,636
- The onions are...
- They're huge.

1949
01:19:26,636 --> 01:19:27,906
no joke.

1950
01:19:28,076 --> 01:19:29,606
Jung Ah, you can't be thinking...

1951
01:19:29,606 --> 01:19:32,076
that these are all we dug up, right?

1952
01:19:32,076 --> 01:19:33,346
Five bags.

1953
01:19:33,506 --> 01:19:34,976
We dug up more than five bags.

1954
01:19:34,976 --> 01:19:36,916
- Really?
- We just filled up five bags.

1955
01:19:36,916 --> 01:19:38,646
Really? You worked so hard.

1956
01:19:38,646 --> 01:19:40,016
(Come here, Sun Young.)

1957
01:19:40,816 --> 01:19:42,156
Jung Ah.

1958
01:19:42,856 --> 01:19:45,356
(At the sound of her cry, Yoon Seo joins.)

1959
01:19:45,356 --> 01:19:46,826
This is what Fresh off the Sea is about.

1960
01:19:46,826 --> 01:19:47,826
(At the sound of her cry, Yoon Seo joins.)

1961
01:19:49,696 --> 01:19:51,496
- You worked hard.
- This is enough.

1962
01:19:52,726 --> 01:19:55,096
(A bitter laugh)

1963
01:19:55,096 --> 01:19:56,766
We bought ice cream.

1964
01:19:56,766 --> 01:19:57,766
- My gosh.
- Would you like one?

1965
01:19:57,766 --> 01:19:59,066
- Yes.
- I'll bring them over.

1966
01:19:59,066 --> 01:20:00,106
I'd like that.

1967
01:20:00,106 --> 01:20:01,276
(The siblings decide to take a short rest.)

1968
01:20:02,176 --> 01:20:06,746
(They sit around on the platform with cool ice cream.)

1969
01:20:08,316 --> 01:20:10,576
(Groaning)

1970
01:20:11,446 --> 01:20:13,916
(Washing his face powerfully)

1971
01:20:14,116 --> 01:20:15,416
What's wrong with him?

1972
01:20:16,256 --> 01:20:18,356
Here. Do this.

1973
01:20:18,356 --> 01:20:19,426
(Here comes a helping hand.)

1974
01:20:19,686 --> 01:20:21,896
(Much more comfortable)

1975
01:20:21,896 --> 01:20:23,156
It's cool.

1976
01:20:23,626 --> 01:20:24,626
My gosh.

1977
01:20:25,096 --> 01:20:27,166
(Washing his face)

1978
01:20:27,526 --> 01:20:30,136
(What a funny guy.)

1979
01:20:31,166 --> 01:20:32,306
Hae Joon.

1980
01:20:32,306 --> 01:20:34,006
(The younger members laugh at his antics.)

1981
01:20:34,406 --> 01:20:36,336
(Tapping his feet to prepare for the next step)

1982
01:20:36,736 --> 01:20:40,946
(Rubbing his foot)

1983
01:20:41,076 --> 01:20:42,176
I've always wanted...

1984
01:20:42,546 --> 01:20:44,016
to do this.

1985
01:20:44,416 --> 01:20:46,886
Washing feet and splashing water on my back...

1986
01:20:46,886 --> 01:20:48,016
by an outdoor faucet.

1987
01:20:48,586 --> 01:20:50,686
It was my dream.

1988
01:20:50,686 --> 01:20:51,686
(The dream of the cool guy)

1989
01:20:51,686 --> 01:20:53,956
When you use a towel,

1990
01:20:54,256 --> 01:20:57,726
wipe your face first, then later your feet.

1991
01:20:58,066 --> 01:21:00,126
Some wipe the feet before the face.

1992
01:21:01,096 --> 01:21:02,536
Who does that?

1993
01:21:03,796 --> 01:21:06,266
(Me.)

1994
01:21:06,966 --> 01:21:09,506
(He wiped his feet with that towel just now.)

1995
01:21:10,276 --> 01:21:11,576
That was nice.

1996
01:21:12,846 --> 01:21:15,216
It feels great after washing up.

1997
01:21:16,446 --> 01:21:17,916
I just need to wash the stuff you bought, right?

1998
01:21:18,086 --> 01:21:20,146
You Seok and I will wash the ingredients.

1999
01:21:20,516 --> 01:21:22,116
Get changed and get some rest.

2000
01:21:22,116 --> 01:21:23,686
(Ordering the three to rest as they're tired from working)

2001
01:21:24,486 --> 01:21:28,596
(You Seok and Jung Ah are in charge of rinsing the ingredients.)

2002
01:21:28,996 --> 01:21:31,466
I'll peel the potatoes. You only need to wash them.

2003
01:21:32,226 --> 01:21:33,226
Okay.

2004
01:21:33,396 --> 01:21:35,766
(Washing the ingredients clean for the curry showdown)

2005
01:21:36,466 --> 01:21:37,666
- Jung Ah.
- Yes?

2006
01:21:37,866 --> 01:21:39,466
- For the curry showdown, - Yes.

2007
01:21:39,766 --> 01:21:42,876
there should be more judges, not just you.

2008
01:21:43,176 --> 01:21:46,146
There should be judges from the production staff...

2009
01:21:46,146 --> 01:21:47,706
- to give scores.
- Let's do that. Okay.

2010
01:21:47,876 --> 01:21:50,076
You'll give 50 points...

2011
01:21:50,076 --> 01:21:51,946
while the other 5 will give 10 points each.

2012
01:21:52,016 --> 01:21:53,416
Let's decide who the winner is that way.

2013
01:21:54,046 --> 01:21:56,686
Jung Ah, what do you think about my idea?

2014
01:21:56,686 --> 01:21:57,716
I like it.

2015
01:21:58,026 --> 01:22:01,086
(How will this decision affect the result of the showdown?)

2016
01:22:02,296 --> 01:22:03,996
Jung Ah, what should I choose for my nickname?

2017
01:22:04,126 --> 01:22:05,996
(Sun Young can't decide on her nickname.)

2018
01:22:05,996 --> 01:22:06,996
Yes? Give me an idea.

2019
01:22:06,996 --> 01:22:09,136
Something fun like Auntie Omakase.

2020
01:22:10,166 --> 01:22:11,866
- How about this, Sun Young?
- What?

2021
01:22:12,566 --> 01:22:14,776
Auntie Curry, instead of Auntie Omakase.

2022
01:22:15,576 --> 01:22:17,636
- Nice. Auntie Curry.
- Auntie Curry.

2023
01:22:18,106 --> 01:22:19,376
Auntie Curry.

2024
01:22:19,376 --> 01:22:20,906
Auntie Curry, not Auntie Omakase.

2025
01:22:20,906 --> 01:22:22,676
Auntie Curry. I love it.

2026
01:22:24,176 --> 01:22:25,486
Was it Auntie?

2027
01:22:26,146 --> 01:22:28,356
- Auntie...
- Auntie Curry.

2028
01:22:28,586 --> 01:22:30,016
Auntie Curry.

2029
01:22:30,586 --> 01:22:32,726
(Laughing)

2030
01:22:32,726 --> 01:22:34,386
- Auntie Curry.
- Auntie Curry.

2031
01:22:34,386 --> 01:22:36,656
- Auntie Curry. Wait.
- No one will understand it.

2032
01:22:36,656 --> 01:22:37,856
I'm Auntie Curry.

2033
01:22:37,856 --> 01:22:39,426
(Saying the name makes them happy)

2034
01:22:39,826 --> 01:22:41,936
I'm so sorry, but can I fart?

2035
01:22:42,436 --> 01:22:43,636
- Do you want me to go out?
- No.

2036
01:22:43,636 --> 01:22:45,106
- Do you want me to scream?
- No, it's going...

2037
01:22:45,106 --> 01:22:46,366
Do you want to fart on the count of three?

2038
01:22:46,666 --> 01:22:48,876
- It went back inside.
- Did it?

2039
01:22:49,676 --> 01:22:51,876
If you can fart in front of each other, you're pretty close.

2040
01:22:52,106 --> 01:22:53,146
Yes.

2041
01:22:54,916 --> 01:22:56,546
Are they prepping the ingredients right now?

2042
01:22:57,546 --> 01:23:00,746
(You Seok is the one making noise.)

2043
01:23:01,656 --> 01:23:04,986
(He's microwaving something.)

2044
01:23:05,586 --> 01:23:09,026
(What is this thing You Seok prepared secretly?)

2045
01:23:09,326 --> 01:23:10,556
- Could you give me one too?
- Yes.

2046
01:23:10,926 --> 01:23:12,196
- Here.
- Thank you.

2047
01:23:12,796 --> 01:23:15,166
Where did it go? Where's my meat?

2048
01:23:15,936 --> 01:23:18,366
(Studying the recipe again for the last time)

2049
01:23:18,636 --> 01:23:19,836
Onions... Gosh.

2050
01:23:21,236 --> 01:23:22,876
I'm all set. Am I?

2051
01:23:24,406 --> 01:23:26,246
Gosh. This will complicate things.

2052
01:23:27,446 --> 01:23:28,746
Goodness.

2053
01:23:28,746 --> 01:23:30,716
(The tension mounts as the showdown gets closer.)

2054
01:23:31,446 --> 01:23:33,986
(Serious)

2055
01:23:35,786 --> 01:23:36,916
Let's go. It's time for war.

2056
01:23:36,916 --> 01:23:39,256
(Let's go. It's time for war.)

2057
01:23:39,561 --> 01:23:42,231
(Can anyone really make curry?)

2058
01:23:42,871 --> 01:23:45,271
(If I cook the way I usually cook, it'll be too good.)

2059
01:23:45,801 --> 01:23:48,871
(I have two secret weapons that I'll use in the end.)

2060
01:23:48,871 --> 01:23:51,511
(I have about 12 secret weapons.)

2061
01:23:52,641 --> 01:23:54,451
This is what a competition show feels like.

2062
01:23:55,451 --> 01:23:57,281
- We'll begin the curry war.
- Yes.

2063
01:23:57,281 --> 01:23:58,481
(Siblings' Showdown: Curry Class Wars)

2064
01:23:58,481 --> 01:24:00,151
It's my first time making curry.

2065
01:24:00,891 --> 01:24:02,321
Do You Know Hidden is my nickname.

2066
01:24:02,721 --> 01:24:04,861
It means, "Do you know about my hidden secrets?"

2067
01:24:05,261 --> 01:24:07,491
I'm going to use coffee powder and yuzu marmalade.

2068
01:24:07,731 --> 01:24:09,491
I've watched the YouTube video many times.

2069
01:24:09,761 --> 01:24:11,561
My goal is to finish the dish well.

2070
01:24:11,831 --> 01:24:12,961
He's my competition.

2071
01:24:15,901 --> 01:24:17,671
What is that? What's that gun?

2072
01:24:18,001 --> 01:24:19,071
Hello.

2073
01:24:19,741 --> 01:24:20,911
I'm Indian Matfia.

2074
01:24:22,571 --> 01:24:23,871
What's up with him?

2075
01:24:24,681 --> 01:24:26,281
What's wrong with him? Seriously.

2076
01:24:26,941 --> 01:24:28,011
What?

2077
01:24:28,351 --> 01:24:29,851
I'm Matfia from India.

2078
01:24:30,481 --> 01:24:33,351
So I can make curry with my eyes closed.

2079
01:24:33,881 --> 01:24:35,251
I'll make it with my eyes shut.

2080
01:24:35,791 --> 01:24:37,351
I'm done with my curry.

2081
01:24:37,351 --> 01:24:38,521
(What's Indian Matfia's secret weapon?)

2082
01:24:38,521 --> 01:24:39,721
Are you confident you'll win?

2083
01:24:40,721 --> 01:24:42,291
If I am not the winner, who will?

2084
01:24:42,291 --> 01:24:43,331
Me.

2085
01:24:43,491 --> 01:24:44,831
- I'll be the winner for sure.
- Me.

2086
01:24:44,831 --> 01:24:46,001
(White Spoon, Auntie Curry)

2087
01:24:46,431 --> 01:24:47,461
Salute!

2088
01:24:47,831 --> 01:24:49,401
I'll do my best today, like always.

2089
01:24:49,801 --> 01:24:51,771
I'm going to make 1990s-style curry...

2090
01:24:51,901 --> 01:24:53,571
that we had when we were young.

2091
01:24:54,441 --> 01:24:56,841
Mine isn't that ordinary.

2092
01:24:57,681 --> 01:24:59,341
- Are you confident of winning?
- Of course.

2093
01:24:59,581 --> 01:25:01,981
I'll show how I take first place for the viewers.

2094
01:25:02,511 --> 01:25:05,121
My dish is called PR for Tongyeong.

2095
01:25:06,021 --> 01:25:08,751
I'm Plump Ravioli With Joons.

2096
01:25:09,921 --> 01:25:13,091
Actually, curry is really easy to make.

2097
01:25:13,391 --> 01:25:15,191
So I want to challenge myself.

2098
01:25:15,691 --> 01:25:18,361
I'm going to add dumplings. It'll be experimental.

2099
01:25:19,301 --> 01:25:21,471
I think this moment...

2100
01:25:21,831 --> 01:25:23,871
will get the highest ratings.

2101
01:25:23,971 --> 01:25:25,141
"Culinary Class Wars?"

2102
01:25:25,471 --> 01:25:26,471
Come on.

2103
01:25:26,471 --> 01:25:28,041
(Curry showdown begins now.)

2104
01:25:28,941 --> 01:25:30,511
- I'm nervous.
- Really?

2105
01:25:30,981 --> 01:25:33,011
- Why?
- I'm not nervous at all.

2106
01:25:33,011 --> 01:25:34,951
(A judge who will evaluate these strong contenders)

2107
01:25:34,951 --> 01:25:36,081
She's here.

2108
01:25:36,551 --> 01:25:37,621
How pretty!

2109
01:25:37,621 --> 01:25:38,621
- Hello.
- Hi.

2110
01:25:38,621 --> 01:25:39,751
- Hello.
- Hello.

2111
01:25:39,751 --> 01:25:41,121
(Head chef of Fresh off the Sea)

2112
01:25:41,121 --> 01:25:42,751
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

2113
01:25:42,951 --> 01:25:44,191
- Yes.
- Chef Yum.

2114
01:25:44,821 --> 01:25:45,921
(It's an honour to meet you.)

2115
01:25:45,921 --> 01:25:48,331
- Okay. Are you all ready?
- Yes.

2116
01:25:48,331 --> 01:25:49,391
Yes, Master.

2117
01:25:49,461 --> 01:25:52,531
In fact, curry is a dish anyone can make.

2118
01:25:52,601 --> 01:25:56,471
The one who makes the curry that the general public likes...

2119
01:25:56,471 --> 01:25:58,401
- will likely win.
- The general public.

2120
01:25:58,901 --> 01:26:01,141
That's what I think.

2121
01:26:01,411 --> 01:26:03,971
The cooking process is really important.

2122
01:26:04,241 --> 01:26:05,311
Are you evaluating the process too?

2123
01:26:05,311 --> 01:26:07,381
That's fundamental to cooking.

2124
01:26:07,611 --> 01:26:09,611
I'll check if the process is hygienic.

2125
01:26:09,611 --> 01:26:11,651
That's going to cost me lots of points.

2126
01:26:11,751 --> 01:26:14,221
You can't eat with your hands while making noise.

2127
01:26:14,221 --> 01:26:16,851
And you can't drool into the food, can you?

2128
01:26:17,091 --> 01:26:18,161
Are you starting now?

2129
01:26:18,161 --> 01:26:20,491
Yes. We'll begin the curry showdown.

2130
01:26:20,491 --> 01:26:21,531
Okay.

2131
01:26:21,661 --> 01:26:23,591
I'll prepare the ingredients first.

2132
01:26:24,261 --> 01:26:26,701
(The participants prepare their ingredients.)

2133
01:26:26,861 --> 01:26:29,071
She said it's important to be hygienic.

2134
01:26:30,431 --> 01:26:31,871
That's mine.

2135
01:26:32,701 --> 01:26:33,871
I'm going to use it.

2136
01:26:34,201 --> 01:26:35,711
You have to be fast.

2137
01:26:35,711 --> 01:26:36,771
Gosh.

2138
01:26:37,271 --> 01:26:38,581
This is a war, indeed.

2139
01:26:38,941 --> 01:26:40,641
What was it? What do I put in?

2140
01:26:40,641 --> 01:26:42,451
Hold on. Salt, sugar, pepper.

2141
01:26:42,981 --> 01:26:45,481
The chef right here is checking the recipe now.

2142
01:26:45,481 --> 01:26:46,651
No, I am not.

2143
01:26:48,051 --> 01:26:50,321
(Chef Yum starts evaluating the participants.)

2144
01:26:50,321 --> 01:26:52,061
(Chopping)

2145
01:26:52,561 --> 01:26:54,121
(Plump Ravioli With Joons is skilfully prepping.)

2146
01:26:57,961 --> 01:26:59,401
Joons seems to have...

2147
01:26:59,401 --> 01:27:01,001
lots of experience cooking.

2148
01:27:01,001 --> 01:27:02,501
Yes. It's...

2149
01:27:02,901 --> 01:27:05,041
I said this was a challenge for myself.

2150
01:27:05,041 --> 01:27:06,141
I said it because...

2151
01:27:06,141 --> 01:27:07,601
But why are your clothes so dirty...

2152
01:27:07,871 --> 01:27:09,671
- when you're cooking?
- That's a point against him!

2153
01:27:09,671 --> 01:27:11,311
- It's...
- That's a deduction.

2154
01:27:11,311 --> 01:27:13,241
- It's possible.
- It's from the onion harvesting.

2155
01:27:13,241 --> 01:27:14,311
But...

2156
01:27:14,681 --> 01:27:17,751
the clothes carry the scent of Geoje Island's onions.

2157
01:27:18,251 --> 01:27:19,251
(Sniffing)

2158
01:27:19,251 --> 01:27:21,321
You do smell like onions.

2159
01:27:21,321 --> 01:27:23,421
I love this onion scent.

2160
01:27:24,021 --> 01:27:27,961
Roughly chop the onions.

2161
01:27:28,861 --> 01:27:30,561
Keep things clean. Am I right?

2162
01:27:30,561 --> 01:27:31,701
Be hygienic.

2163
01:27:31,701 --> 01:27:33,261
You always have to cook in a clean environment.

2164
01:27:33,261 --> 01:27:34,771
I'll throw away the piece that fell from the board.

2165
01:27:35,001 --> 01:27:37,101
Okay. Did you see that, Judge Yum?

2166
01:27:38,371 --> 01:27:40,171
I won't use anything that falls off the cutting board.

2167
01:27:41,141 --> 01:27:42,141
(Yoon Seo's wind shield)

2168
01:27:42,141 --> 01:27:43,211
Goodness.

2169
01:27:43,511 --> 01:27:44,711
I'll help you.

2170
01:27:45,181 --> 01:27:46,481
I'll help you with it.

2171
01:27:48,481 --> 01:27:50,181
I'll do it myself, Sun Young.

2172
01:27:50,181 --> 01:27:51,581
No, I'll do it.

2173
01:27:52,221 --> 01:27:53,251
Is it...

2174
01:27:53,251 --> 01:27:55,191
- a deduction...
- No.

2175
01:27:55,191 --> 01:27:56,521
- not to get help from others?
- No.

2176
01:27:56,521 --> 01:27:58,761
- No.
- Those who know how to be loved...

2177
01:27:58,761 --> 01:28:01,391
- also know how to love.
- I didn't want to burden you.

2178
01:28:02,161 --> 01:28:03,191
Okay.

2179
01:28:04,761 --> 01:28:06,401
Judge, I'm doing okay with the hygiene so far, right?

2180
01:28:06,401 --> 01:28:07,871
- Yes. It's totally hygienic.
- Gosh.

2181
01:28:07,871 --> 01:28:09,331
- Don't worry about it now.
- Gosh.

2182
01:28:09,771 --> 01:28:10,901
I didn't lose points.

2183
01:28:10,901 --> 01:28:12,741
If I don't lose points for being dirty, I'll win it.

2184
01:28:12,741 --> 01:28:13,841
She's writing something.

2185
01:28:13,841 --> 01:28:15,211
(Deducting points for spitting from talking too much)

2186
01:28:15,211 --> 01:28:16,241
Good.

2187
01:28:17,411 --> 01:28:19,811
(Meanwhile...)

2188
01:28:19,981 --> 01:28:21,881
What is this? Was it pre-cooked?

2189
01:28:21,881 --> 01:28:23,551
I haven't cooked it yet. I just microwaved it...

2190
01:28:23,551 --> 01:28:24,781
to dry it out.

2191
01:28:25,251 --> 01:28:27,051
- I see.
- I need to caramelize it.

2192
01:28:27,051 --> 01:28:28,951
- And it'll take too long.
- Gosh. I'm going to do that too.

2193
01:28:28,951 --> 01:28:30,521
- Otherwise, it'll take too long.
- Okay.

2194
01:28:30,521 --> 01:28:31,921
(His strategy to reduce the caramelizing time)

2195
01:28:32,261 --> 01:28:34,461
(Meticulous planning skills)

2196
01:28:35,391 --> 01:28:36,831
(Staring)

2197
01:28:37,261 --> 01:28:39,431
(Shaking)

2198
01:28:39,431 --> 01:28:40,831
(Glancing)

2199
01:28:42,301 --> 01:28:43,931
Do your hands shake like that all the time?

2200
01:28:45,001 --> 01:28:46,601
I have shaky hands.

2201
01:28:46,601 --> 01:28:47,841
- Shaky hands.
- Yes.

2202
01:28:49,471 --> 01:28:50,641
Shaky hands.

2203
01:28:51,711 --> 01:28:53,911
You Seok is well-prepared.

2204
01:28:53,911 --> 01:28:55,211
He microwaved the onions.

2205
01:28:55,851 --> 01:28:57,181
I should've done that too.

2206
01:28:58,381 --> 01:29:00,581
To let the moisture out easily,

2207
01:29:00,581 --> 01:29:02,421
I was told to chop it against the grain.

2208
01:29:02,421 --> 01:29:03,751
- Okay. Sure.
- Yes.

2209
01:29:04,121 --> 01:29:05,891
(Uneven)

2210
01:29:06,991 --> 01:29:08,591
Have you made curry before?

2211
01:29:08,591 --> 01:29:10,131
This is my first time making it.

2212
01:29:10,131 --> 01:29:11,661
- It's your first time.
- Really?

2213
01:29:11,661 --> 01:29:12,761
- Yes.
- That's minus five points.

2214
01:29:13,001 --> 01:29:15,371
- She has never made it before?
- That's possible.

2215
01:29:15,371 --> 01:29:16,601
- That's possible.
- She's young.

2216
01:29:16,601 --> 01:29:17,771
That's totally possible.

2217
01:29:17,941 --> 01:29:19,171
Get cooked quickly.

2218
01:29:19,301 --> 01:29:20,401
(What's the recipe...)

2219
01:29:20,401 --> 01:29:21,871
(for Do You Know Hidden's first curry in her life?)

2220
01:29:22,811 --> 01:29:24,241
Stir-fry the meat.

2221
01:29:24,471 --> 01:29:26,481
I'll stir-fry the onions in the oil from the meat.

2222
01:29:27,211 --> 01:29:29,151
I heard that would bring out the flavours.

2223
01:29:29,911 --> 01:29:32,251
Unlike others, I'll use cabbage.

2224
01:29:32,781 --> 01:29:34,421
I'll add a little bit of sugar.

2225
01:29:34,421 --> 01:29:37,321
That will speed up the caramelization.

2226
01:29:37,451 --> 01:29:39,691
They're still a little moist, but they are tasty as is.

2227
01:29:40,191 --> 01:29:42,431
Also, there are two kicks to the recipe.

2228
01:29:42,791 --> 01:29:44,291
- Are you going to kick me?
- Yes.

2229
01:29:44,291 --> 01:29:45,601
I'm going to kick him.

2230
01:29:45,601 --> 01:29:46,861
(Playing along with his joke)

2231
01:29:47,031 --> 01:29:48,031
(Laughing)

2232
01:29:48,031 --> 01:29:49,031
Get it!

2233
01:29:49,031 --> 01:29:51,101
Now, I'll add the curry.

2234
01:29:52,171 --> 01:29:53,701
(Auntie Curry is the first to add the curry blocks.)

2235
01:29:53,701 --> 01:29:54,811
Let's dissolve it.

2236
01:29:55,471 --> 01:29:58,341
I've never used this brand before.

2237
01:29:58,511 --> 01:30:00,781
I don't know how it'll turn out.

2238
01:30:01,511 --> 01:30:03,011
Actually, when I cook at home,

2239
01:30:03,351 --> 01:30:05,951
I don't stir-fry them in butter.

2240
01:30:06,451 --> 01:30:10,891
I usually use avocado oil to be healthy.

2241
01:30:10,891 --> 01:30:13,291
But today, I'm using butter to focus only on the taste.

2242
01:30:14,461 --> 01:30:17,461
There is something I have to do personally.

2243
01:30:18,261 --> 01:30:19,331
That's...

2244
01:30:19,831 --> 01:30:21,471
- What?
- an ordinary curry, isn't it?

2245
01:30:21,731 --> 01:30:23,831
It's the curry dads cook on Sundays.

2246
01:30:24,401 --> 01:30:26,941
- Mine isn't that ordinary.
- Is that so?

2247
01:30:26,941 --> 01:30:28,041
- Okay.
- Does it have a kick to it?

2248
01:30:28,041 --> 01:30:29,571
- Of course.
- Okay.

2249
01:30:29,641 --> 01:30:30,711
Don't worry.

2250
01:30:30,711 --> 01:30:32,311
- You'll add apples.
- That's right.

2251
01:30:32,641 --> 01:30:34,311
I always add apples.

2252
01:30:34,311 --> 01:30:36,651
If I put too much butter,

2253
01:30:36,651 --> 01:30:38,281
it can be too greasy.

2254
01:30:38,881 --> 01:30:40,181
It's because I'm adding cheese too.

2255
01:30:40,581 --> 01:30:42,321
Apples and cheese are my secrets.

2256
01:30:42,321 --> 01:30:43,421
(She'll make to sweet and flavourful with apples and cheese.)

2257
01:30:43,421 --> 01:30:44,591
Apples make it tastier.

2258
01:30:44,861 --> 01:30:48,061
Actually, I add water at this stage.

2259
01:30:48,391 --> 01:30:51,331
I don't stir-fry it for too long. I let it simmer.

2260
01:30:51,801 --> 01:30:54,201
Once the ingredients are cooked, I'm done.

2261
01:30:54,731 --> 01:30:57,271
(Is Auntie Curry's dish finished just like that?)

2262
01:30:58,141 --> 01:31:00,201
(In the meantime,)

2263
01:31:00,801 --> 01:31:03,741
(Plump Ravioli With Joons is cooking smoothly too.)

2264
01:31:03,741 --> 01:31:04,941
(Glancing)

2265
01:31:05,611 --> 01:31:07,081
(MSG)

2266
01:31:07,281 --> 01:31:08,281
(Glancing)

2267
01:31:10,951 --> 01:31:13,581
(Adding MSG without looking, unbeknownst to Chef Yum)

2268
01:31:14,251 --> 01:31:16,151
(He dreams of a perfect crime.)

2269
01:31:16,151 --> 01:31:17,221
(Chef Yum approaches.)

2270
01:31:17,721 --> 01:31:19,021
(Nervous)

2271
01:31:19,021 --> 01:31:20,591
- Did you add meat?
- Pardon?

2272
01:31:20,591 --> 01:31:21,831
- Meat?
- I don't add meat.

2273
01:31:21,831 --> 01:31:23,531
- It doesn't include meat.
- No, it doesn't.

2274
01:31:27,261 --> 01:31:29,201
(Laughing out of relief)

2275
01:31:29,531 --> 01:31:30,971
Wait, what was I going to do?

2276
01:31:32,371 --> 01:31:34,041
(Staring)

2277
01:31:34,811 --> 01:31:36,671
I'll microwave something downstairs.

2278
01:31:36,671 --> 01:31:37,841
(To thaw the dumplings)

2279
01:31:39,141 --> 01:31:40,181
Why am I...

2280
01:31:40,641 --> 01:31:41,781
(Running)

2281
01:31:42,551 --> 01:31:44,211
(He's busy all by himself.)

2282
01:31:46,581 --> 01:31:48,551
(Getting ready to thaw them right away)

2283
01:31:49,121 --> 01:31:50,151
I'm feeling dizzy.

2284
01:31:51,051 --> 01:31:52,061
I'm dizzy.

2285
01:31:52,621 --> 01:31:55,731
(Zoning out)

2286
01:31:56,061 --> 01:31:57,131
A Korean melon?

2287
01:31:58,801 --> 01:31:59,801
A Korean melon.

2288
01:32:00,901 --> 01:32:01,971
A Korean melon.

2289
01:32:01,971 --> 01:32:05,341
(Rinsing the Korean melon as if he thought of something)

2290
01:32:06,201 --> 01:32:07,801
(Suddenly, it makes him curious.)

2291
01:32:08,241 --> 01:32:09,941
Do I need to try this hard?

2292
01:32:11,811 --> 01:32:13,981
Do I not need to do this?

2293
01:32:15,111 --> 01:32:16,581
Do I not need to do this?

2294
01:32:18,521 --> 01:32:20,521
Okay. Jumping.

2295
01:32:22,551 --> 01:32:24,761
(Joons finally made it back.)

2296
01:32:24,761 --> 01:32:25,921
Gosh. Water needs to boil.

2297
01:32:25,921 --> 01:32:27,561
(Another problem)

2298
01:32:28,691 --> 01:32:30,091
Gosh. This is exhausting.

2299
01:32:30,091 --> 01:32:31,901
(Returning)

2300
01:32:32,731 --> 01:32:34,971
(While Joons is scurrying around frantically...)

2301
01:32:35,401 --> 01:32:36,571
I'm done with my dish.

2302
01:32:37,301 --> 01:32:39,771
- Really? Are you finished?
- That's all.

2303
01:32:40,171 --> 01:32:41,341
A lot needs to be cooked.

2304
01:32:42,411 --> 01:32:44,141
- That's all I needed to do.
- What's that?

2305
01:32:45,311 --> 01:32:46,811
(He has a bucketful of ingredients that need to be cooked.)

2306
01:32:47,181 --> 01:32:49,411
- That's 90 percent of my cooking.
- That looks good.

2307
01:32:50,911 --> 01:32:52,251
